/* Lecture 2 video lecture data: chapters, slide start times, timed bilingual transcript (generated). */
window.LEC = {"num":2,"videoBase":"https://wabyking.github.io/nlp2026-videos-1/lecture02/","parts":[{"n":1,"title":"What Is Linguistics? Structure, Grammar and Englishes","blurb":"Why structure underlies how we understand and use language, what grammaticality means, and why attitude matters more than accent when speaking English.","file":"lecture02_part1.mp4","poster":"lecture02_part1.jpg","duration":575.9,"first":1,"last":22,"slides":[{"n":1,"title":"Title: Lecture 2","t":0.8},{"n":2,"title":"Word sequence","t":30.97},{"n":3,"title":"Today's Plan","t":57.63},{"n":4,"title":"What is linguistics?","t":86.52},{"n":5,"title":"What is linguistics?","t":107.71},{"n":6,"title":"How do we understand language?","t":134.17},{"n":7,"title":"An important insight","t":156.78},{"n":8,"title":"There’s structure underlying language","t":181.73},{"n":9,"title":"Structure dictates how we can use language","t":207.1},{"n":10,"title":"Structure dictates how we can use language","t":229.9},{"n":11,"title":"Structure dictates how we can use language","t":251.87},{"n":12,"title":"Structure dictates how we can use language","t":273.07},{"n":13,"title":"Structure dictates how we can use language","t":295.02},{"n":14,"title":"Structure dictates how we can use language","t":316.15},{"n":15,"title":"Recall the rules you learned in school","t":338.53},{"n":16,"title":"Grammaticality","t":364.73},{"n":17,"title":"Example","t":390.59},{"n":18,"title":"Who Speaks English?","t":417.43},{"n":19,"title":"Local English or Standard English","t":445.52},{"n":20,"title":"When speaking a new language, what matters most is your attitude — not your accuracy (including vocabulary and grammar)","t":476.24},{"n":21,"title":"脸皮越厚，英语越溜","t":501.51},{"n":22,"title":"Linguistics as a Tool for Analysis","t":552.86}],"sents":[[0.8,6.8,1,"Hi everyone, and welcome to Lecture two: Linguistics Basics and Word Representations.","大家好，欢迎来到第二讲：语言学基础与词表示。"],[7.15,14.26,1,"Today we'll look at two things: how language is put together, and how we turn words into numbers that a model can use.","今天我们要看两件事：语言是如何组织起来的，以及我们如何把词变成模型能用的数字。"],[14.61,18.43,1,"We'll start with familiar sentences and small examples.","我们会从熟悉的句子和小例子开始。"],[18.78,21.93,1,"Then we'll build up to word vectors and word2vec.","然后逐步过渡到词向量和 word2vec。"],[22.28,29.57,1,"By the end, you should be able to explain why context helps us learn meaning, and what the model is actually learning.","学完之后，你应该能解释为什么上下文能帮助我们学习词义，以及模型实际上在学什么。"],[30.82,32.17,2,"Here's the big picture.","先来看整体图景。"],[32.52,36.19,2,"In the older pipeline, we analyze the language in stages.","在过去的流水线方法中，我们分阶段地分析语言。"],[36.54,42.13,2,"We might identify words, build a syntax tree, and then use that structure for a task.","我们可能先识别词，再构建句法树，然后把这种结构用于具体任务。"],[42.48,47.27,2,"With an end-to-end model, much of that work happens inside one model.","而在端到端模型中，这些工作大多在一个模型内部完成。"],[47.62,49.68,2,"So where did the linguistic structure go?","那么，语言结构到哪里去了？"],[50.03,51.8,2,"Keep that question in mind.","请把这个问题记在心里。"],[52.15,56.23,2,"We'll come back to this diagram after exploring the structure itself.","在探讨完语言结构本身之后，我们会再回到这张图。"],[57.48,59.18,3,"Here's our route for today.","这是我们今天的路线。"],[59.53,64.78,3,"First, we'll move from characters to words, sentences, and discourse.","首先，我们从字符出发，依次讲到词、句子和篇章。"],[65.13,69.01,3,"Then we'll look at how NLP models represent words.","然后我们看 NLP 模型如何表示词。"],[69.36,71.46,3,"The connection is context.","把两部分连起来的是上下文。"],[71.81,78.84,3,"We'll see how context helps us interpret language, and then how it gives us a training signal for word vectors.","我们会看到上下文如何帮助我们理解语言，以及它如何为词向量提供训练信号。"],[79.19,81.3,3,"You don't need to memorize every label.","你不需要记住每一个术语。"],[81.65,85.12,3,"Focus on what each idea helps us explain.","重点关注每个概念能帮我们解释什么。"],[86.37,88.03,4,"Let's start with linguistics.","我们从语言学开始。"],[88.38,91.14,4,"What comes to mind when you hear that word?","听到这个词，你会想到什么？"],[91.49,93.75,4,"Grammar is probably one answer.","语法大概是其中一个答案。"],[94.1,99.83,4,"But linguistics also looks at word formation, meaning, and how people use language together.","但语言学还研究构词、意义，以及人们如何共同使用语言。"],[100.18,106.31,4,"For this course, we'll focus on the parts that help us understand text and build NLP systems.","在这门课里，我们关注那些有助于理解文本、构建 NLP 系统的部分。"],[107.56,110.91,5,"Linguistics is the scientific study of language.","语言学是对语言的科学研究。"],[111.26,115.58,5,"It asks what patterns speakers use and how those patterns work.","它关心说话者使用哪些规律，以及这些规律如何运作。"],[115.93,122.25,5,"For example, why does one word order sound natural while another sounds strange?","比如，为什么一种词序听起来自然，另一种却很别扭？"],[122.6,125.43,5,"And how could a computer learn that difference?","计算机又怎样才能学会这种差别？"],[125.78,130.43,5,"Those questions connect human language understanding with NLP.","这些问题把人类的语言理解和 NLP 联系了起来。"],[130.78,132.77,5,"Let's begin with the human side.","我们先从人的一侧说起。"],[134.02,138.1,6,"Think about this sentence: The small robot opened the red door.","想想这个句子：那个小机器人打开了红色的门。"],[138.45,143.06,6,"You've probably never heard that exact sentence before, but you understand it right away.","你可能从没听过一模一样的句子，但你立刻就能理解它。"],[143.41,146.55,6,"You know the words, and you know how they fit together.","你认识这些词，也知道它们如何组合在一起。"],[146.9,150.73,6,"Their arrangement tells you who did the action and what happened.","词的排列告诉你是谁做了这个动作、发生了什么。"],[151.08,155.38,6,"That ability to combine familiar pieces is what we'll look at next.","这种把熟悉的部件组合起来的能力，就是我们接下来要看的。"],[156.63,159.43,7,"Language has structure at several levels.","语言在多个层次上都有结构。"],[159.78,161.29,7,"Take Alice talked to Bob.","以 Alice talked to Bob 为例。"],[161.64,166.22,7,"We can look at the individual characters, the word talked, or the whole sentence.","我们可以看单个字符、单词 talked，或者整个句子。"],[166.57,171.64,7,"Inside talked, talk gives us the action and the ending marks the past tense.","在 talked 内部，talk 表示动作，词尾则标记过去时。"],[171.99,176.51,7,"At the sentence level, we can work out who talked and to whom.","在句子层面，我们能弄清是谁在说话、对谁说。"],[176.86,180.33,7,"Each level gives us a different kind of information.","每个层次都提供了不同类型的信息。"],[181.58,186.81,8,"Compare Isabel broke the window with The window was broken by Isabel.","比较 Isabel broke the window 和 The window was broken by Isabel。"],[187.16,191.25,8,"The word order changes, but Isabel is still the person who broke it.","词序变了，但打破窗户的人依然是 Isabel。"],[191.6,194.83,8,"Now look at the made-up words, plid and plof.","再看这两个编造的词：plid 和 plof。"],[195.18,196.55,8,"We don't know what they mean.","我们并不知道它们是什么意思。"],[196.9,200.93,8,"Even so, the sentence structure helps us follow their roles.","即便如此，句子结构仍能帮我们看出它们各自的角色。"],[201.28,205.7,8,"So we already know something about the sentence before we know every word.","所以，在认识每个词之前，我们就已经对句子有所了解。"],[206.95,211.5,9,"Let's try a small experiment with The cat sat on the mat.","我们用 The cat sat on the mat 这个句子做个小实验。"],[211.85,215.59,9,"Which parts could we replace with a shorter expression?","哪些部分可以用更短的表达来替换？"],[215.94,218.12,9,"Keep an eye on the branches of the tree.","请留意这棵树的分支。"],[218.47,222.37,9,"Over the next few slides, we'll replace different groups of words.","接下来几页，我们会替换不同的词组。"],[222.72,228.5,9,"If a group behaves as a unit, that gives us evidence that it forms a constituent.","如果一组词表现得像一个整体，就说明它构成一个成分。"],[229.75,232.18,10,"First, replace the cat with he.","首先，把 the cat 替换成 he。"],[232.53,234.86,10,"We get He sat on the mat.","得到 He sat on the mat。"],[235.21,238.46,10,"That sounds fine, assuming we're talking about a male cat.","听起来没问题，假设我们说的是一只公猫。"],[238.81,243.94,10,"The two words the cat work together as the subject noun phrase.","the cat 这两个词合起来充当主语名词短语。"],[244.29,246.74,10,"We can replace the whole group with a pronoun.","我们可以用一个代词替换整个词组。"],[247.09,250.47,10,"Now let's try the same idea on the other side of the sentence.","现在在句子的另一侧试试同样的做法。"],[251.72,254.03,11,"This time, replace the mat with it.","这次，把 the mat 替换成 it。"],[254.38,256.89,11,"We get The cat sat on it.","得到 The cat sat on it。"],[257.24,260.01,11,"Notice that on stays where it is.","注意，on 仍留在原处。"],[260.36,264.82,11,"So the part we're replacing is just the noun phrase the mat.","所以我们替换的只是名词短语 the mat。"],[265.17,268.18,11,"It's a small unit inside the larger phrase on the mat.","它是更大短语 on the mat 内部的一个小单位。"],[268.53,271.67,11,"On the next slide, we'll replace that larger unit.","下一页，我们来替换那个更大的单位。"],[272.92,275.37,12,"Now replace on the mat with there.","现在把 on the mat 替换成 there。"],[275.72,278.57,12,"We get The cat sat there.","得到 The cat sat there。"],[278.92,281.45,12,"This time, on disappears too.","这一次，on 也一起消失了。"],[281.8,283.99,12,"That's the difference from the previous slide.","这就是和上一页的区别。"],[284.34,288.49,12,"It replaced the mat, while there replaces the whole location phrase.","it 替换的是 the mat，而 there 替换的是整个表示地点的短语。"],[288.84,293.62,12,"These two replacements help us see the smaller group sitting inside the larger one.","这两次替换帮我们看到，小的词组嵌在大的词组里面。"],[294.87,296.52,13,"We can go one step further.","我们还可以更进一步。"],[296.87,300.82,13,"Imagine saying, The dog sat on the mat, and the cat did so too.","想象这样说：The dog sat on the mat, and the cat did so too。"],[301.17,303.76,13,"Did so stands for sat on the mat.","did so 代替的是 sat on the mat。"],[304.11,305.91,13,"It replaces the whole verb phrase.","它替换了整个动词短语。"],[306.26,309.66,13,"We've now found several groups that behave as units.","现在我们已经找到了好几个表现为整体的词组。"],[310.01,314.75,13,"Let's see what happens when we choose a group that doesn't fit the tree.","来看看如果选一个不符合这棵树的词组，会发生什么。"],[316.0,320.48,14,"Here we try to replace the cat sat and leave on the mat behind.","这里我们试着替换 the cat sat，而把 on the mat 留下。"],[320.83,322.27,14,"It doesn't work in the same way.","这样就行不通了。"],[322.62,327.43,14,"Those words sit next to each other, but they don't form a constituent in this tree.","这几个词虽然相邻，但在这棵树中并不构成一个成分。"],[327.78,331.66,14,"That's why a sentence needs more than a left-to-right word list.","这就是为什么句子不只是一个从左到右的词列表。"],[332.01,334.1,14,"Its internal groupings matter too.","句子内部的分组同样重要。"],[334.45,337.13,14,"Next, we'll connect this to grammaticality.","接下来，我们把这一点和合语法性联系起来。"],[338.38,342.39,15,"Some rules we learn at school are really advice about writing style.","我们在学校学的一些规则，其实是关于写作风格的建议。"],[342.74,347.22,15,"For example, a teacher may tell you to avoid starting a sentence with And.","比如，老师可能会让你不要用 And 开头写句子。"],[347.57,349.16,15,"People still do it naturally.","但人们还是会很自然地这么用。"],[349.51,354.23,15,"Linguistics asks a broader question: what patterns do speakers actually use?","语言学问的是一个更广的问题：说话者实际上在使用哪些规律？"],[354.58,357.13,15,"We also have to consider the language variety.","我们还必须考虑语言变体。"],[357.48,363.33,15,"A pattern that is normal in one dialect may differ from formal standard English.","在某种方言中很正常的用法，可能与正式的标准英语不同。"],[364.58,370.16,16,"Grammaticality is about whether a sentence follows the patterns of a particular language variety.","合语法性指的是一个句子是否符合某种特定语言变体的规律。"],[370.51,373.5,16,"Take Colorless green ideas sleep furiously.","看这个句子：Colorless green ideas sleep furiously。"],[373.85,377.59,16,"The structure sounds like English, even though the meaning is odd.","它的结构听起来像英语，尽管意思很奇怪。"],[377.94,382.16,16,"And a sentence with a grammatical mistake can still be understandable.","而一个有语法错误的句子，仍然可能被理解。"],[382.51,389.19,16,"So when we judge a sentence, we should separate its structure from its meaning and its usefulness in communication.","所以评判一个句子时，我们应把它的结构、意义和交际作用区分开来。"],[390.44,391.62,17,"Look at I love her.","看 I love her 这个句子。"],[391.97,395.13,17,"I is the subject, and her is the object.","I 是主语，her 是宾语。"],[395.48,399.6,17,"Standard English uses different pronoun forms in those positions.","标准英语在这两个位置上使用不同形式的代词。"],[399.95,402.89,17,"In Me love she, those forms don't fit.","在 Me love she 中，这些形式就不对了。"],[403.24,407.47,17,"In Me a cupcake ate, the usual word order changes too.","在 Me a cupcake ate 中，连通常的词序也变了。"],[407.82,409.85,17,"We can still guess the message.","但我们仍然能猜出意思。"],[410.2,416.03,17,"That shows why understanding an intention and judging grammatical form are different tasks.","这说明理解意图和判断语法形式是两项不同的任务。"],[417.28,424.15,18,"English is used by many different communities, including people who speak it as an additional language.","英语被许多不同的群体使用，其中包括把它作为第二语言的人。"],[424.5,427.87,18,"Those communities develop their own patterns.","这些群体会形成各自的语言规律。"],[428.22,431.62,18,"For NLP, that raises a practical issue.","对 NLP 来说，这带来一个现实问题。"],[431.97,435.24,18,"A model trained on one variety may struggle with another.","在一种变体上训练的模型，可能难以处理另一种变体。"],[435.59,440.58,18,"The figure here motivates that point; we don't need an exact percentage from it.","这张图是为了说明这一点，我们不需要从中读出确切的百分比。"],[440.93,444.12,18,"Let's look at an example of local English.","我们来看一个本地英语的例子。"],[445.37,450.68,19,"Here is a short talk about local English and standard English, linked on the slide.","这是一段关于本地英语和标准英语的简短演讲，链接就在幻灯片上。"],[451.03,455.94,19,"As you watch it, listen for one difference between local English and standard English.","观看时，请留意本地英语和标准英语之间的一个差别。"],[456.29,461.5,19,"It could be a word, a pronunciation, or a way of expressing something.","它可能是一个词、一种发音，或一种表达方式。"],[461.85,465.58,19,"Notice the question in the caption: embrace or replace?","注意字幕里的问题：接纳，还是替换？"],[465.93,469.19,19,"Some choices depend on the audience and setting.","有些选择取决于听众和场合。"],[469.54,474.84,19,"An NLP system has to deal with that variation in real conversations.","NLP 系统必须在真实对话中应对这种差异。"],[476.09,478.22,20,"Suppose you don't know the word kettle.","假设你不知道 kettle，也就是水壶这个词。"],[478.57,481.67,20,"You might say, the thing we use to boil water.","你可能会说：就是我们用来烧水的那个东西。"],[482.02,483.91,20,"The other person can still help you.","对方仍然能帮到你。"],[484.26,490.06,20,"That's part of language ability too: finding another way to express an idea and clearing up confusion.","这也是语言能力的一部分：换一种方式表达想法，并消除误解。"],[490.41,494.39,20,"Accuracy helps, but communication also involves working together.","准确固然有帮助，但沟通也需要双方合作。"],[494.74,500.11,20,"A useful conversational system needs to handle that kind of imperfect input.","一个有用的对话系统需要能处理这种不完美的输入。"],[501.36,507.67,21,"Here's the same message from someone close to home: Professor Xu Yangsheng, the founding president of our university.","同样的道理，也来自我们身边的人：我们学校的创校校长徐扬生教授。"],[508.02,513.23,21,"The Chinese line says, roughly, the thicker your skin, the more fluent your English.","左边这句中文大意是：脸皮越厚，英语越溜。"],[513.58,521.5,21,"In this clip, he says that learning English takes a thick skin, and that you should be an interesting person.","在这段视频里，他说学英语一定要脸皮厚，还要做一个有趣的人。"],[521.85,527.89,21,"He also says that the ability to express yourself is a tool you'll rely on for the rest of your life.","他还说，表达能力是你一辈子生存的工具。"],[528.24,533.82,21,"So don't wait until your grammar is perfect before you speak up, in English or in this class.","所以，不要等到语法完美了才开口，无论是说英语，还是在这门课上。"],[534.17,539.24,21,"Ask the question, try the sentence, and let the other person help you repair it.","大胆提问，大胆说出句子，让对方帮你把它修正好。"],[539.59,545.64,21,"That's exactly the kind of messy, cooperative communication we saw in the last two clips.","这正是我们在前两段视频中看到的那种不完美但相互配合的沟通。"],[545.99,551.46,21,"With that, let's wrap up the motivation and turn linguistics into a tool for analysis.","动机部分就讲到这里，下面我们把语言学变成一种分析工具。"],[552.71,560.53,22,"So far, we've used linguistics to explain patterns, test sentence structure, and think about language variation.","到目前为止，我们用语言学解释了规律、检验了句子结构，也思考了语言变体。"],[560.88,564.07,22,"Now we'll organize those ideas more systematically.","现在我们把这些想法更系统地组织起来。"],[564.42,569.21,22,"We'll move through the levels of language and ask what information each level adds.","我们会逐一走过语言的各个层次，看看每个层次增加了什么信息。"],[569.56,574.06,22,"That will give us a clearer picture of what an NLP model needs to learn.","这能让我们更清楚 NLP 模型需要学习什么。"]]},{"n":2,"title":"From Characters to Words: Scripts, Unicode, Tokens","blurb":"Levels of linguistic structure starting from characters: Chinese writing, character encodings and Unicode, then tokenization, Zipf's law and the out-of-vocabulary problem.","file":"lecture02_part2.mp4","poster":"lecture02_part2.jpg","duration":827.0,"first":23,"last":49,"slides":[{"n":23,"title":"Today's Plan","t":0.8},{"n":24,"title":"Levels of Linguistic Structure","t":25.13},{"n":25,"title":"Linguistic Structure in NLP","t":49.06},{"n":26,"title":"Character","t":72.38},{"n":27,"title":"Pictograph 象形文字","t":97.4},{"n":28,"title":"Ideographic characters （表意文字）","t":121.15},{"n":29,"title":"Chinese Characters","t":144.3},{"n":30,"title":"Large Number of Characters","t":180.43},{"n":31,"title":"Traditional and Simplified Characters","t":228.72},{"n":32,"title":"Variant Characters","t":262.23},{"n":33,"title":"Dialect Characters and Dialectal Use of Standard Characters","t":285.96},{"n":34,"title":"Character Encoding Standards","t":313.28},{"n":35,"title":"Character Encoding Standards","t":350.01},{"n":36,"title":"Unicode","t":381.49},{"n":37,"title":"Unicode","t":422.25},{"n":38,"title":"Example","t":460.35},{"n":39,"title":"Linguistic Structure in NLP","t":489.18},{"n":40,"title":"From Characters to Words","t":511.14},{"n":41,"title":"Yes or No？","t":532.63},{"n":42,"title":"Words aren’t just deﬁned by blanks","t":568.34},{"n":43,"title":"Tokenization","t":599.89},{"n":44,"title":"Spelling variants, typos, etc.","t":631.81},{"n":45,"title":"How Many Different Words Are There in English?","t":658.59},{"n":46,"title":"Zipf’s law: the long tail","t":690.76},{"n":47,"title":"How Many Different Words Are There in English?","t":723.29},{"n":48,"title":"Word Mapping","t":752.07},{"n":49,"title":"Motivating challenge: out-of-vocabulary (OOV) words","t":791.95}],"sents":[[0.8,2.02,23,"Here's the next section.","接下来是新的一部分。"],[2.37,8.09,23,"We'll start with characters and work upward to words, sentences, and discourse.","我们从字符开始，逐级向上讲到词、句子和篇章。"],[8.44,11.4,23,"At each level, think about what could go wrong.","在每个层次上，想一想可能会出什么错。"],[11.75,14.24,23,"A decoding error changes the characters.","解码错误会改变字符。"],[14.59,16.99,23,"A segmentation error changes the words.","分词错误会改变词。"],[17.34,20.13,23,"A parsing error changes who did what.","句法分析错误会改变谁做了什么。"],[20.48,23.73,23,"These are practical reasons to understand the structure.","这些都是理解语言结构的现实理由。"],[24.98,27.85,24,"This hierarchy gives us a useful map.","这个层级结构为我们提供了一张有用的地图。"],[28.2,33.6,24,"Characters form words, words form sentences, and sentences connect into discourse.","字符组成词，词组成句子，句子再连接成篇章。"],[33.95,36.49,24,"In real use, the levels interact.","在实际使用中，这些层次是相互作用的。"],[36.84,40.29,24,"A later sentence can clarify an earlier word, for example.","比如，后面的句子可以澄清前面某个词的意思。"],[40.64,44.09,24,"But it's easier to study them one at a time first.","不过，先一个一个地研究会更容易。"],[44.44,47.66,24,"Let's begin with the written symbols we store in a computer.","我们先从存储在计算机里的书写符号开始。"],[48.91,51.64,25,"We'll look at characters from two angles.","我们从两个角度来看字符。"],[51.99,55.91,25,"First, what does a character contribute to the writing system?","第一，字符对书写系统有什么贡献？"],[56.26,58.48,25,"Second, how do we store it digitally?","第二，我们如何以数字形式存储它？"],[58.83,65.29,25,"Chinese gives us useful examples because a character often carries both sound and meaning.","汉字提供了很好的例子，因为一个汉字往往同时承载读音和意义。"],[65.64,70.98,25,"After that, we'll look at encoding and the bytes that represent the text.","之后，我们再看编码，以及表示文本的字节。"],[72.23,80.33,26,"English has twenty-six basic letters, but English text also includes numbers, punctuation, and other symbols.","英语有二十六个基本字母，但英文文本里还有数字、标点和其他符号。"],[80.68,83.83,26,"Chinese has a much larger character inventory.","汉字的字符集则要大得多。"],[84.18,87.3,26,"A character also doesn't have a fixed size in bytes.","一个字符占多少字节也不是固定的。"],[87.65,89.72,26,"That depends on the encoding.","这取决于所用的编码。"],[90.07,96.0,26,"Keep the written symbol and its storage format separate as we go through these examples.","看这些例子时，请把书写符号本身和它的存储格式区分开。"],[97.25,104.39,27,"Here you can see how some character shapes relate to objects such as the sun, a mountain, or a tree.","这里可以看到，一些汉字的字形与太阳、山、树等事物相关。"],[104.74,108.75,27,"The older forms sometimes make that link easier to see.","较古老的字形有时更容易看出这种联系。"],[109.1,110.52,27,"These are pictographs.","这些就是象形字。"],[110.87,115.5,27,"They're a useful starting point, but only a small part of the Chinese writing system.","它们是很好的切入点，但只占汉字系统的一小部分。"],[115.85,119.75,27,"Most characters involve more than a simple picture of an object.","大多数汉字都不只是对事物的简单描画。"],[121.0,125.17,28,"These examples show how parts of a character can suggest a concept.","这些例子展示了汉字的组成部分如何暗示一个概念。"],[125.52,131.57,28,"For instance, xiu, meaning rest, is often explained as a person leaning beside a tree.","比如“休”，意思是休息，常被解释为一个人倚靠在树旁。"],[131.92,134.03,28,"That's a helpful classroom illustration.","这是一个很好的课堂示例。"],[134.38,138.8,28,"We shouldn't treat every such explanation as a full history of the character, though.","不过，我们不应把每个这类解释都当成这个字完整的演变历史。"],[139.15,142.9,28,"The next table shows several ways characters can be formed.","下一张表展示了汉字的几种构成方式。"],[144.15,150.05,29,"The largest group here is semantic-phonetic compounds, more than ninety percent of characters.","这里最大的一类是形声字，占汉字的百分之九十以上。"],[150.4,155.64,29,"One part gives a clue about meaning, and another gives a clue about sound.","一部分提示意义，另一部分提示读音。"],[155.99,162.35,29,"Compare the characters for clear, feeling, and please, all pronounced qing.","比较表示清澈、感情和请求的“清”“情”“请”，它们都读 qing。"],[162.7,166.89,29,"You can see a shared component, while the other component changes.","你能看到它们有一个共同的部件，而另一个部件在变化。"],[167.24,173.49,29,"These clues aren't perfect, but they can help a model connect a rare character with more familiar ones.","这些线索并不完美，但能帮助模型把生僻字和更常见的字联系起来。"],[173.84,179.03,29,"As the bottom line says, a Chinese character often represents a morpheme and a syllable.","正如底部这句话所说，一个汉字通常代表一个语素和一个音节。"],[180.28,186.24,30,"There are many Chinese characters, but a relatively small number account for most everyday text.","汉字数量很多，但相对少量的字就占了日常文本的绝大部分。"],[186.59,195.87,30,"The Kangxi dictionary of seventeen sixteen lists over forty-seven thousand characters, and a nineteen ninety-four dictionary lists over eighty-seven thousand.","1716 年的《康熙字典》收录了四万七千多个字，1994 年的《中华字海》收录了八万七千多个。"],[196.22,205.18,30,"Yet one thousand characters may cover ninety-two percent of written material, and three thousand cover more than ninety-nine percent.","然而，一千个字可能覆盖百分之九十二的书面材料，三千个字则覆盖百分之九十九以上。"],[205.53,207.45,30,"The rest form a long tail.","其余的字构成一条长尾。"],[207.8,210.63,30,"The coverage figures depend on the text collection.","这些覆盖率数字取决于所用的文本集合。"],[210.98,216.66,30,"The storage point is simpler: eight bits give us only two hundred fifty-six combinations.","存储方面的道理更简单：八位只能表示二百五十六种组合。"],[217.01,221.15,30,"Sixteen bits give us sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-six.","十六位可以表示六万五千五百三十六种。"],[221.5,227.32,30,"Modern character coverage goes beyond that, which is why encoding needs careful design.","现代字符集的规模超出了这个范围，所以编码需要精心设计。"],[228.57,233.32,31,"Simplified and traditional Chinese don't always have a one-to-one mapping.","简体字和繁体字之间并不总是一一对应的。"],[233.67,241.24,31,"Simplified characters are used in mainland China, Singapore, and Malaysia, and traditional ones in Taiwan, Hong Kong, and Macau.","简体字用于中国大陆、新加坡和马来西亚，繁体字用于台湾、香港和澳门。"],[241.59,245.51,31,"One simplified character can correspond to several traditional forms.","一个简体字可能对应多个繁体字形。"],[245.86,254.27,31,"For example, the simplified gan can map to different traditional characters, depending on whether we mean dryness or doing something.","比如，简体的“干”可以对应不同的繁体字，取决于指的是“干燥”还是“做事”。"],[254.62,257.35,31,"A converter needs context to choose correctly.","转换工具需要借助上下文才能选对。"],[257.7,260.83,31,"Even this basic cleanup task can involve meaning.","即便是这样基础的清洗任务，也会牵涉到意义。"],[262.08,266.56,32,"Variant characters have different written forms for closely related usage.","异体字是指用法相近、但写法不同的字。"],[266.91,271.8,32,"You may encounter them in old texts, names, or regional writing.","你可能会在古籍、人名或地方性书写中遇到它们。"],[272.15,275.17,32,"For search, grouping variants together can help.","对检索来说，把异体字归并在一起会有帮助。"],[275.52,280.39,32,"For a historical document or someone's name, the original form may matter.","但对于历史文献或人名，原本的字形可能很重要。"],[280.74,284.56,32,"So normalization depends on what we're trying to do with the text.","所以，规范化要看我们想用这些文本做什么。"],[285.81,292.13,33,"Regional varieties may use their own characters, or use familiar characters in different ways.","地方变体可能使用自己的字，或者以不同方式使用常见的字。"],[292.48,297.7,33,"Look at the examples in these tables, from Cantonese, Shanghainese, and Southern Min.","看看这几张表中的例子，分别来自粤语、上海话和闽南语。"],[298.05,305.11,33,"A Mandarin-based system might treat an unfamiliar form as a mistake, even when it's perfectly normal locally.","基于普通话的系统可能会把不熟悉的写法当成错误，即使它在当地完全正常。"],[305.46,308.93,33,"This is the same coverage issue we saw with English varieties.","这和我们在英语变体中看到的覆盖问题是一样的。"],[309.28,311.88,33,"Next, let's see how the characters are stored.","接下来，我们看看这些字是如何存储的。"],[313.13,318.82,34,"GB twenty-three twelve is an early encoding standard for simplified Chinese.","GB2312 是一个早期的简体中文编码标准。"],[319.17,327.2,34,"It covers seven thousand four hundred forty-five characters, including six thousand seven hundred sixty-three simplified Chinese characters.","它收录了 7445 个字符，其中包括 6763 个简体汉字。"],[327.55,332.21,34,"The main idea is an agreed mapping between characters and bytes.","核心思想是在字符和字节之间建立一个约定好的映射。"],[332.56,334.86,34,"The encoder writes a sequence of bytes.","编码器写出一串字节。"],[335.21,338.95,34,"The decoder must use the same rules to read it back.","解码器必须用同样的规则把它读回来。"],[339.3,341.69,34,"You don't need to memorize the bit patterns here.","这里的比特规则不需要死记。"],[342.04,348.61,34,"Remember the agreement between the two sides, because a mismatch causes unreadable text.","要记住的是双方的约定，因为一旦不匹配，就会出现乱码。"],[349.86,354.86,35,"GBK expanded the earlier character coverage, adding traditional characters.","GBK 扩展了早期的字符覆盖范围，加入了繁体字。"],[355.21,363.36,35,"GB eighteen-oh-thirty, released in two thousand, expanded it further and supports both simplified and traditional characters.","2000 年发布的 GB18030 进一步扩展，同时支持简体和繁体汉字。"],[363.71,367.96,35,"The version details on this slide provide historical context.","这页上的版本细节是作为历史背景提供的。"],[368.31,373.61,35,"If you've opened a text file and seen strange symbols, the wrong decoder may have been the problem.","如果你打开文本文件时看到一堆奇怪的符号，问题可能就出在用错了解码方式。"],[373.96,377.76,35,"A broader standard helps systems exchange text more reliably.","更通用的标准能让系统之间更可靠地交换文本。"],[378.11,380.09,35,"That brings us to Unicode.","这就引出了 Unicode。"],[381.34,386.62,36,"Unicode is one standard meant to unify all the earlier encoding standards.","Unicode 是一个旨在统一此前所有编码标准的标准。"],[386.97,389.13,36,"It gives characters code points.","它为每个字符分配一个码位。"],[389.48,394.86,36,"For example, the letter A has the code point U plus zero zero four one.","比如，字母 A 的码位是 U+0041。"],[395.21,398.26,36,"Think of the code point as an ID for the character.","可以把码位看作字符的身份编号。"],[398.61,404.59,36,"UTF-8 and UTF-16 tell us how to write that ID as bytes.","UTF-8 和 UTF-16 则规定如何把这个编号写成字节。"],[404.94,409.49,36,"So Unicode identifies the character, and the encoding controls its storage.","所以，Unicode 负责标识字符，编码方式负责控制存储。"],[409.84,412.49,36,"A code point is a little like a contact's ID.","码位有点像通讯录里联系人的编号。"],[412.84,418.87,36,"Different file formats can store that ID differently without changing which contact it refers to.","不同的文件格式可以用不同方式存储这个编号，但它指向的仍是同一个联系人。"],[419.22,420.85,36,"Let's compare those encodings.","我们来比较一下这些编码方式。"],[422.1,428.78,37,"Unicode is an industry standard with more than one hundred thousand characters, implemented by different encodings.","Unicode 是一个收录了十万多个字符的行业标准，由不同的编码方式来实现。"],[429.13,434.92,37,"UTF-8 uses one byte for ASCII characters and up to four bytes for other code points.","UTF-8 对 ASCII 字符用一个字节，对其他码位最多用四个字节。"],[435.27,440.23,37,"UTF-16 generally uses two or four bytes.","UTF-16 一般使用两个或四个字节。"],[440.58,447.76,37,"There's one more detail: a symbol you see on screen can contain several code points, as with some accented letters or emoji.","还有一个细节：屏幕上看到的一个符号可能包含多个码位，比如某些带重音的字母或表情符号。"],[448.11,453.02,37,"So bytes, code points, and visible characters aren't always counted the same way.","所以，字节数、码位数和可见字符数并不总是一样的。"],[453.37,458.95,37,"Han unification also doesn't mean that simplified and traditional forms are all merged.","另外，汉字统一并不意味着简体和繁体字形全都被合并了。"],[460.2,463.63,38,"The phrase here means natural language processing.","这里的词组意思是自然语言处理。"],[463.98,467.45,38,"Pick one character and compare its bytes down the three rows.","选一个字，沿着三行比较它的字节。"],[467.8,471.28,38,"The character stays the same, but the byte sequence changes.","字没有变，但字节序列变了。"],[471.63,477.02,38,"In GB twenty-three twelve it takes two bytes, while in UTF-8 it takes three.","在 GB2312 中它占两个字节，而在 UTF-8 中占三个。"],[477.37,481.1,38,"That's why we need to know the encoding before reading the bytes.","这就是为什么在读取字节之前必须知道编码。"],[481.45,487.78,38,"Once we've decoded the text correctly, we can move to a new question: how do we divide it into words?","正确解码文本之后，我们就可以转向一个新问题：如何把它切分成词？"],[489.03,490.68,39,"Now we're at the word level.","现在我们来到词这一层。"],[491.03,496.21,39,"English spaces give us a useful clue, but they don't solve every case.","英语中的空格是有用的线索，但并不能解决所有情况。"],[496.56,499.98,39,"Chinese usually gives us no spaces between words at all.","而中文的词与词之间通常根本没有空格。"],[500.33,503.52,39,"We'll first look at what makes word boundaries difficult.","我们先看看是什么让词边界难以确定。"],[503.87,509.74,39,"Then we'll see how subword tokenization handles the problem in a practical way.","然后看子词切分如何以一种实用的方式解决这个问题。"],[510.99,512.47,40,"Look again at talked.","再看一下 talked。"],[512.82,517.91,40,"The stem talk gives us the action, and the ending adds grammatical information.","词干 talk 表示动作，词尾则增加了语法信息。"],[518.26,522.39,40,"If a model can connect talk and talked, it can share evidence between them.","如果模型能把 talk 和 talked 联系起来，就能在两者之间共享证据。"],[522.74,525.03,40,"That's one reason to study morphology.","这就是研究形态学的一个理由。"],[525.38,531.23,40,"But before we analyze what's inside a word, we have to decide where the word begins and ends.","但在分析词的内部之前，我们得先确定一个词从哪里开始、到哪里结束。"],[532.48,535.23,41,"Could we just split English text at spaces?","能不能直接按空格切分英文文本？"],[535.58,540.35,41,"It works reasonably well until we hit examples like New York or doesn't.","这在多数情况下还行，直到遇到 New York 或 doesn't 这样的例子。"],[540.7,543.67,41,"New York has a space but names one place.","New York 中间有空格，却指的是一个地方。"],[544.02,548.43,41,"Doesn't has no space but contains a verb and a negation.","doesn't 没有空格，却包含一个动词和一个否定。"],[548.78,558.08,41,"There are more puzzles too: abbreviations, hyphenated forms like New York-based, and names like R2-D2.","还有更多难题：缩写、像 New York-based 这样带连字符的形式，以及 R2-D2 这样的名字。"],[558.43,563.16,41,"Chinese adds a different challenge: several words appear in one continuous string.","中文带来另一种挑战：好几个词连成一串出现。"],[563.51,566.94,41,"So we'll need a more explicit tokenization rule.","所以我们需要更明确的分词规则。"],[568.19,572.08,42,"Compare ice cream, website, and web site.","比较 ice cream、website 和 web site。"],[572.43,575.7,42,"Spacing doesn't give us a consistent measure of meaning.","空格并不能稳定地反映意义单位。"],[576.05,581.19,42,"Now add Chinese text without spaces, plus contractions and clitics.","再加上没有空格的中文文本，以及缩约形式和附着词。"],[581.54,588.47,42,"The Italian example, dirglielo, packs tell, him, and it into one written word.","意大利语的例子 dirglielo，把“告诉”“他”“它”压进了一个书面词里。"],[588.82,592.7,42,"We can't expect one simple whitespace rule to handle everything.","我们不能指望一条简单的空格规则搞定一切。"],[593.05,598.49,42,"A tokenizer needs to make clear, repeatable choices that match the model's training data.","分词器需要做出清晰、可重复、并与模型训练数据一致的选择。"],[599.74,606.18,43,"The Penn Treebank example uses a particular set of rules for punctuation and contractions.","Penn Treebank 的例子对标点和缩约形式采用了一套特定的规则。"],[606.53,612.35,43,"Look at the output: doesn't is split into does plus a separate negation piece.","看输出：doesn't 被拆成 does 和一个单独的否定部分。"],[612.7,615.45,43,"Another tokenizer might make different choices.","另一个分词器可能会做出不同的选择。"],[615.8,619.08,43,"What matters for a trained system is consistency.","对一个训练好的系统来说，关键在于一致性。"],[619.43,625.73,43,"If training splits doesn't into two tokens but inference keeps it as one, the inputs no longer match.","如果训练时把 doesn't 切成两个 token，推理时却保留为一个，输入就对不上了。"],[626.08,630.41,43,"These preprocessing choices become part of the model's setup.","这些预处理选择会成为模型设定的一部分。"],[631.66,635.23,44,"Lowercasing can merge Cat, cat, and CAT.","转成小写可以把 Cat、cat 和 CAT 合并起来。"],[635.58,638.46,44,"That's useful when those forms mean the same thing.","当这些形式意思相同时，这很有用。"],[638.81,645.34,44,"But US and us show the risk: lowercasing can remove a distinction we need.","但 US 和 us 说明了风险：转小写可能抹掉我们需要的区分。"],[645.69,649.26,44,"Labor and labour give us another kind of variation.","labor 和 labour 则是另一种变体。"],[649.61,654.85,44,"Every normalization choice affects both the information we keep and the size of the vocabulary.","每一种规范化选择，都会影响我们保留的信息和词表的大小。"],[655.2,657.19,44,"Let's look at that size next.","接下来我们看看词表的大小。"],[658.44,661.09,45,"First, separate tokens from types.","首先，区分词例和词型。"],[661.44,667.82,45,"In the cat chased the cat, we have five tokens, but only three types: the, cat, and chased.","在 the cat chased the cat 中，有五个词例，但只有三个词型：the、cat 和 chased。"],[668.17,677.8,45,"The Google N-gram corpus, for instance, has one trillion tokens and thirteen million word types that appear at least forty times.","比如 Google N-gram 语料库有一万亿个词例，出现至少四十次的词型有一千三百万个。"],[678.15,683.5,45,"As we collect more text, we keep finding new types, including rare names and spellings.","随着收集的文本越来越多，我们会不断发现新的词型，包括罕见的名字和拼写。"],[683.85,685.71,45,"So how many English words are there?","那么，英语到底有多少个词？"],[686.06,689.36,45,"It depends on what we count and which text we look at.","这取决于我们数的是什么，以及看的是哪些文本。"],[690.61,693.88,46,"Zipf's law describes a very uneven distribution.","齐普夫定律描述了一种非常不均匀的分布。"],[694.23,701.12,46,"The r-th most common word has a probability roughly proportional to one over r.","第 r 个最常见的词，其概率大致与 r 分之一成正比。"],[701.47,703.43,46,"A few words appear all the time.","少数几个词总是出现。"],[703.78,705.36,46,"Many others appear rarely.","而其他许多词则很少出现。"],[705.71,709.29,46,"As you move along the rank axis, frequency drops.","沿着排名轴往右走，频率不断下降。"],[709.64,717.31,46,"More data gives us lots of evidence for the common words, but many rare words still have very little evidence.","更多数据能为常见词提供大量证据，但许多罕见词的证据仍然很少。"],[717.66,721.89,46,"This long tail is one of the main challenges for word representations.","这条长尾是词表示面临的主要挑战之一。"],[723.14,727.48,47,"Do take, takes, and took count as three words or one?","take、takes 和 took，算三个词还是一个词？"],[727.83,731.35,47,"A corpus type count may treat them separately.","按语料库词型计数，可能会把它们分开算。"],[731.7,736.07,47,"A dictionary-based count may group them under the lemma take.","按词典计数，则可能把它们归到词元 take 之下。"],[736.42,744.76,47,"That's why everyday vocabulary estimates, like adults knowing about thirty thousand words, and corpus counts can look very different.","这就是为什么像“成年人大约认识三万个词”这样的日常估计，和语料库统计看起来差别很大。"],[745.11,750.67,47,"Grouping related forms can also help a model, which leads to word mapping.","把相关的形式归并起来也能帮助模型，这就引出了词映射。"],[751.92,754.59,48,"There are several ways to group word forms.","归并词形有几种方法。"],[754.94,758.58,48,"Normalization standardizes spelling or case.","规范化统一拼写或大小写。"],[758.93,762.29,48,"Lemmatization maps a form to a dictionary entry.","词形还原把一个词形映射到词典条目。"],[762.64,765.77,48,"Stemming uses simpler rules to remove endings.","词干提取则用更简单的规则去掉词尾。"],[766.12,768.19,48,"Cats to cat is easy.","从 cats 到 cat 很容易。"],[768.54,770.32,48,"Went to go needs more knowledge.","从 went 到 go 就需要更多知识。"],[770.67,774.75,48,"A stemmer can also produce a result that isn't a real word.","词干提取器还可能得到一个并不是真实单词的结果。"],[775.1,780.96,48,"If we reduce both organize and organization to a shared stem, we may improve matching.","如果把 organize 和 organization 都归到同一个词干，可能会提高匹配效果。"],[781.31,785.88,48,"But we also lose the difference between an action and a noun.","但我们也会失去动作和名词之间的区别。"],[786.23,790.55,48,"The method we choose depends on how much information we need.","选择哪种方法，取决于我们需要保留多少信息。"],[791.8,799.49,49,"Many NLP systems assume a fixed vocabulary, but still have to handle out-of-vocabulary, or OOV, words.","许多 NLP 系统假设词表是固定的，但仍要处理未登录词，也就是 OOV 词。"],[799.84,803.83,49,"Suppose our vocabulary contains dog and cat, but not dogs and cats.","假设词表里有 dog 和 cat，但没有 dogs 和 cats。"],[804.18,808.21,49,"Those plural forms may become unknown tokens.","这些复数形式就可能变成未知 token。"],[808.56,813.16,49,"In this example, several different words end up with the same OOV symbol.","在这个例子里，几个不同的词最后都变成了同一个 OOV 符号。"],[813.51,815.47,49,"We lose useful distinctions.","我们丢失了有用的区分。"],[815.82,818.67,49,"Morphology can help connect related forms.","形态学可以帮助把相关的形式联系起来。"],[819.02,825.2,49,"Later, subword tokenization will let us represent a new word as smaller pieces.","稍后，子词切分会让我们把新词表示成更小的片段。"]]},{"n":3,"title":"Morphemes, Chinese Words and Byte Pair Encoding","blurb":"How words are built from morphemes, why Chinese word segmentation is hard, how GPT-style models use byte pair encoding to handle unseen words, and parts of speech.","file":"lecture02_part3.mp4","poster":"lecture02_part3.jpg","duration":416.3,"first":50,"last":63,"slides":[{"n":50,"title":"Review: Linguistic structure","t":0.8},{"n":51,"title":"Morphemes (语素): stems, afﬁxes","t":26.76},{"n":52,"title":"Chinese Words","t":55.82},{"n":53,"title":"Chinese Words","t":76.76},{"n":54,"title":"Chinese Word Formation","t":109.03},{"n":55,"title":"Challenges in Chinese: Limited Morphological Marking","t":143.54},{"n":56,"title":"Challenges in Chinese: Word Ambiguity","t":174.16},{"n":57,"title":"How Do State-of-the-Art Models Represent Words?","t":208.23},{"n":58,"title":"Tokenization in GPT-Style Models","t":231.62},{"n":59,"title":"Byte Pair Encoding in GPT-Style Models","t":260.65},{"n":60,"title":"Byte Pair Encoding for Tokenization","t":294.42},{"n":61,"title":"Byte Pair Encoding Handles OOV Words","t":322.32},{"n":62,"title":"Parts of Speech","t":357.29},{"n":63,"title":"Parts of Speech","t":388.81}],"sents":[[0.8,3.97,50,"Books can be a plural noun, as in three books.","books 可以是复数名词，比如 three books。"],[4.32,7.77,50,"It can also be a verb, as in She books a room.","它也可以是动词，比如 She books a room。"],[8.12,11.92,50,"Both map to the lemma book, but their grammatical features differ.","两者都对应词元 book，但语法特征不同。"],[12.27,15.99,50,"A morphological analyzer tries to keep that information.","形态分析器会尽量保留这些信息。"],[16.34,25.36,50,"The surrounding sentence helps it choose the right analysis, especially in highly inflected languages like Czech, Finnish, or Turkish.","周围的句子能帮它选出正确的分析，在捷克语、芬兰语、土耳其语这类屈折变化丰富的语言中尤其如此。"],[26.61,31.04,51,"A morpheme is a small unit with meaning or a grammatical function.","语素是带有意义或语法功能的最小单位。"],[31.39,37.06,51,"In disgracefully, we can identify dis, grace, ful, and ly.","在 disgracefully 中，我们可以识别出 dis、grace、ful 和 ly。"],[37.41,40.5,51,"Grace is the stem, and it can stand alone.","grace 是词干，它可以单独使用。"],[40.85,43.8,51,"The other pieces usually need a host.","其他部分通常需要依附于别的成分。"],[44.15,47.92,51,"Adding or removing them changes the meaning or word class.","加上或去掉它们，会改变词义或词类。"],[48.27,51.99,51,"So one written word can contain several useful pieces.","所以一个书面词可以包含好几个有用的部分。"],[52.34,54.42,51,"Let's compare that with Chinese.","我们来和中文比较一下。"],[55.67,58.75,52,"So far, we've mainly used English examples.","到目前为止，我们主要用的是英语例子。"],[59.1,61.32,52,"Chinese gives us a different picture.","中文则呈现出不同的面貌。"],[61.67,67.84,52,"There are usually no spaces between words, and many grammatical distinctions don't appear as word endings.","词与词之间通常没有空格，许多语法区别也不体现在词尾上。"],[68.19,72.57,52,"The meaning is still there, but the system often needs to look elsewhere for it.","意义依然存在，但系统往往需要到别处去寻找它。"],[72.92,75.36,52,"Let's start with what counts as a Chinese word.","我们先来看什么才算一个中文词。"],[76.61,80.95,53,"A Chinese character and a Chinese word aren't always the same thing.","汉字和汉语的词并不总是一回事。"],[81.3,87.28,53,"A character can stand alone in one expression and appear inside a larger word in another.","一个字可以在某个表达中单独使用，在另一个表达中又成为更大的词的一部分。"],[87.63,93.79,53,"Also, the meaning of a compound may not follow directly from its individual characters.","而且，复合词的意义未必能直接从单个字推出来。"],[94.14,100.57,53,"For example, da ren means an adult, while xiao ren usually means a petty person, not just a small one.","比如，“大人”指成年人，而“小人”通常指品行不端的人，而不只是个子小的人。"],[100.92,104.19,53,"Translating one character at a time can miss the point.","逐字翻译可能会抓不住重点。"],[104.54,107.63,53,"We need to look at how the whole expression is used.","我们需要看整个表达是如何使用的。"],[108.88,114.02,54,"Chinese forms words through compounds, affixation, and reduplication.","中文通过复合、加缀和重叠来构词。"],[114.37,118.36,54,"Compounds can have two, three, or even four syllables.","复合词可以有两个、三个甚至四个音节。"],[118.71,121.37,54,"Repetition is especially easy to notice.","重叠尤其容易被注意到。"],[121.72,127.1,54,"For example, shangliang shangliang can suggest discussing something briefly or giving it a try.","比如“商量商量”，可以表示简单商量一下，或者试着商量看看。"],[127.45,129.53,54,"The repeated form adds meaning.","重叠形式本身就增加了意义。"],[129.88,138.33,54,"One caution with the affix examples: a character isn't automatically a prefix or suffix just because it comes first or last.","关于加缀的例子要注意：一个字并不因为出现在开头或结尾，就自动成为前缀或后缀。"],[138.68,142.14,54,"We have to examine its role in that expression.","我们必须考察它在那个表达中的作用。"],[143.39,147.92,55,"Compare I was a student, I am a student, and I will be a student.","比较 I was a student、I am a student 和 I will be a student。"],[148.27,150.59,55,"English changes the verb construction.","英语改变的是动词结构。"],[150.94,157.97,55,"Chinese can express these differences with time expressions and context while leaving the main predicate unchanged.","中文可以通过时间词和上下文来表达这些差别，而主要谓语保持不变。"],[158.32,164.78,55,"The slide shows the same pattern for person, number, and case: Chinese leaves them unmarked on the word itself.","幻灯片显示，人称、数和格也是同样的情况：中文并不在词本身上标记它们。"],[165.13,167.74,55,"So less inflection doesn't mean less meaning.","所以，屈折变化少并不意味着意义少。"],[168.09,172.76,55,"It means the clues appear in different places, and the model has to find them.","而是说线索出现在别的地方，模型需要把它们找出来。"],[174.01,177.26,56,"Here's a useful example: ping pang qiu pai mai wan le.","来看一个很有用的例子：“乒乓球拍卖完了”。"],[177.61,180.79,56,"One reading says the table-tennis paddles are sold out.","一种理解是：乒乓球拍卖完了，也就是球拍售罄了。"],[181.14,185.98,56,"Another grouping reads pai mai as auction, so the ping-pong balls have been auctioned off.","另一种切分把“拍卖”连在一起，意思就变成乒乓球已经拍卖完了。"],[186.33,189.37,56,"Where we put the word boundary changes the reading.","词边界放在哪里，决定了句子的意思。"],[189.72,196.72,56,"The slide lists more cases, from lexical ambiguity to overlapping and combinatorial segmentation ambiguity.","幻灯片列出了更多情况，从词汇歧义到交集型和组合型的切分歧义。"],[197.07,199.2,56,"More context helps us decide.","更多的上下文能帮助我们做出判断。"],[199.55,206.83,56,"This raises a practical question: does a model need perfect word segmentation before it can learn anything useful?","这就提出了一个现实问题：模型是否必须先有完美的分词，才能学到有用的东西？"],[208.08,210.41,57,"Many models use subword tokens.","许多模型使用子词 token。"],[210.76,216.93,57,"A common word may stay in one piece, while an unfamiliar word splits into several pieces.","常见词可能保持为一个整体，而陌生的词会被拆成几个片段。"],[217.28,220.65,57,"Those pieces don't have to match dictionary words or morphemes.","这些片段不必对应词典里的词或语素。"],[221.0,225.29,57,"They just need to give the model a useful, consistent input.","它们只需要为模型提供有用且一致的输入。"],[225.64,230.22,57,"Let's look at what that produces in an actual tokenized string.","我们来看看在实际切分后的字符串中是什么样子。"],[231.47,233.68,58,"Each colored span is a token.","每一个彩色片段就是一个 token。"],[234.03,237.76,58,"Notice that tokens don't line up perfectly with words or characters.","注意，token 并不和词或字符完全对齐。"],[238.11,242.62,58,"This passage has ninety-seven tokens and two hundred forty-eight characters.","这段文字有 97 个 token、248 个字符。"],[242.97,247.92,58,"You may hear a rough estimate of four characters per token for English.","你可能听说过，英文大约每个 token 四个字符。"],[248.27,251.14,58,"It's only a rough guide for certain tokenizers.","这只是针对某些分词器的粗略参考。"],[251.49,254.87,58,"It doesn't work equally well for Chinese or every model.","它对中文、对每个模型并不同样适用。"],[255.22,259.25,58,"The vocabulary and splitting rules determine the result.","结果取决于词表和切分规则。"],[260.5,265.28,59,"Byte Pair Encoding, or BPE, learns frequent pieces from text.","字节对编码，也就是 BPE，从文本中学习高频片段。"],[265.63,270.48,59,"It's a practical middle ground between character-level and word-level modeling.","它是字符级和词级建模之间的一个实用折中。"],[270.83,274.39,59,"It starts small and repeatedly merges common neighboring units.","它从小单位开始，反复合并常见的相邻单位。"],[274.74,280.16,59,"For example, if t and h often occur together, they might become th.","比如，如果 t 和 h 经常一起出现，它们就可能合并成 th。"],[280.51,283.19,59,"Later merges can create larger pieces.","后续的合并可以产生更大的片段。"],[283.54,288.64,59,"Common sequences get compact representations, while rare ones can still be split.","常见序列得到紧凑的表示，而罕见序列仍然可以被拆开。"],[288.99,293.02,59,"Next, let's separate learning those rules from using them.","接下来，我们把学习这些规则和使用这些规则区分开。"],[294.27,295.87,60,"There are two stages here.","这里有两个阶段。"],[296.22,301.66,60,"First, the token learner builds the vocabulary and merge rules from a training corpus.","首先，token 学习器从训练语料中构建词表和合并规则。"],[302.01,306.64,60,"Then the token segmenter applies those fixed rules to new text.","然后，token 切分器把这些固定的规则应用到新文本上。"],[306.99,312.06,60,"Words such as play, playing, and played might share a piece like play.","像 play、playing 和 played 这样的词，可能共享 play 这个片段。"],[312.41,317.34,60,"That's useful, but BPE doesn't promise a perfect linguistic analysis.","这很有用，但 BPE 并不保证得到完美的语言学分析。"],[317.69,320.92,60,"It follows frequency patterns in the training text.","它遵循的是训练文本中的频率规律。"],[322.17,325.09,61,"Byte-level BPE starts with bytes.","字节级 BPE 从字节开始。"],[325.44,332.0,61,"With all two hundred fifty-six byte values available, a new name can always fall back to smaller pieces.","既然全部 256 个字节值都可用，一个新名字总能退回到更小的片段来表示。"],[332.35,337.21,61,"That means we can represent the input even when there's no whole-word token for it.","这意味着即使没有对应的整词 token，我们也能表示这个输入。"],[337.56,341.29,61,"But representing a name doesn't mean understanding it.","但能表示一个名字，并不等于理解了它。"],[341.64,349.09,61,"It's like being able to spell a new name letter by letter: you can write it down perfectly and still know nothing about the person.","这就像能把一个新名字逐个字母拼出来：你能把它写得分毫不差，却对这个人一无所知。"],[349.44,353.6,61,"We've solved a coverage problem; learning its meaning still needs evidence.","我们解决了覆盖问题，但要学到它的含义，仍然需要证据。"],[353.95,355.89,61,"Now let's return to grammar.","现在我们回到语法。"],[357.14,361.0,62,"Parts of speech describe what words do in a sentence.","词性描述的是词在句子中起什么作用。"],[361.35,366.7,62,"In This is a simple sentence, simple is an adjective and sentence is a noun.","在 This is a simple sentence 中，simple 是形容词，sentence 是名词。"],[367.05,373.93,62,"Each word also gets a short tag, like D T for determiner and J J for adjective.","每个词还会有一个简短的标签，比如限定词是 DT，形容词是 JJ。"],[374.28,376.95,62,"Now compare read a book with book a room.","再比较 read a book 和 book a room。"],[377.3,379.93,62,"The same spelling has different roles.","同样的拼写，角色却不同。"],[380.28,383.81,62,"So a word's category can depend on its context.","所以一个词的类别可能取决于它的上下文。"],[384.16,387.41,62,"That becomes important when we build sentence structure.","这一点在构建句子结构时会变得很重要。"],[388.66,390.71,63,"You don't need to memorize this whole table.","你不需要记住这整张表。"],[391.06,392.54,63,"Let's decode a few tags.","我们来解读几个标签。"],[392.89,398.38,63,"N N is a singular noun, and N N S is a plural noun.","NN 是单数名词，NNS 是复数名词。"],[398.73,403.07,63,"The different verb tags distinguish forms such as the past tense.","不同的动词标签区分了诸如过去时这样的形式。"],[403.42,408.81,63,"More detailed labels carry more information, but they're also harder to assign.","标签越细，携带的信息越多，但也越难标注。"],[409.16,414.49,63,"Once we have these word-level categories, we can start grouping words into phrases.","有了这些词级类别，我们就可以开始把词组合成短语了。"]]},{"n":4,"title":"Sentences and Discourse: Parse Trees to Coherence","blurb":"Sentence structure through phrase-structure trees, dependency parsing and parsing ambiguity, then discourse structure, coherence relations and discourse parsing across sentences.","file":"lecture02_part4.mp4","poster":"lecture02_part4.jpg","duration":637.0,"first":64,"last":87,"slides":[{"n":64,"title":"Linguistic Structure in NLP","t":0.8},{"n":65,"title":"Phrase","t":25.9},{"n":66,"title":"English Phrase","t":55.68},{"n":67,"title":"Phrase Structure Grammar","t":84.15},{"n":68,"title":"Parsing and Syntax Analysis","t":108.76},{"n":69,"title":"Parse tree (Syntactic Tree)","t":139.83},{"n":70,"title":"Parse tree (Syntactic Tree)","t":160.35},{"n":71,"title":"Parse tree (Syntactic Tree)","t":180.3},{"n":72,"title":"Parse tree (Syntactic Tree)","t":201.27},{"n":73,"title":"Parse tree (Syntactic Tree)","t":223.51},{"n":74,"title":"Parse tree (Syntactic Tree)","t":246.21},{"n":75,"title":"Parse tree (Syntactic Tree)","t":267.7},{"n":76,"title":"Dependency Syntax","t":292.1},{"n":77,"title":"Dependency Structure","t":321.98},{"n":78,"title":"Dependency Parsing","t":355.65},{"n":79,"title":"Parsing Ambiguity","t":380.99},{"n":80,"title":"Parsing Ambiguity","t":403.45},{"n":81,"title":"Linguistic Structure in NLP","t":435.72},{"n":82,"title":"Discourse Structure","t":458.25},{"n":83,"title":"Discourse Processing","t":485.08},{"n":84,"title":"Discourse Coherence Relations","t":509.15},{"n":85,"title":"Additional Discourse Relations","t":539.12},{"n":86,"title":"Discourse Parsing","t":571.91},{"n":87,"title":"Coherence Models","t":602.98}],"sents":[[0.8,3.67,64,"Knowing the words alone still leaves something out.","只认识这些词，仍然会遗漏一些东西。"],[4.02,11.39,64,"The dog chased the cat and The cat chased the dog contain exactly the same words.","The dog chased the cat 和 The cat chased the dog 包含完全相同的词。"],[11.74,14.07,64,"But different animals are doing the chasing.","但追赶的是不同的动物。"],[14.42,18.29,64,"Sentence structure connects the words to their roles.","句子结构把词和它们的角色联系起来。"],[18.64,24.5,64,"We'll look at two ways to represent that structure: phrase trees and dependency relations.","我们会看两种表示这种结构的方法：短语结构树和依存关系。"],[25.75,32.14,65,"In The girl saw the hedgehog, the girl forms one noun phrase and the hedgehog forms another.","在 The girl saw the hedgehog 中，the girl 构成一个名词短语，the hedgehog 构成另一个。"],[32.49,34.97,65,"Saw the hedgehog forms a verb phrase.","saw the hedgehog 构成一个动词短语。"],[35.32,39.61,65,"These groups are the kinds of units we tested with substitutions earlier.","这些词组正是我们之前用替换法检验过的那类单位。"],[39.96,42.9,65,"A phrase can be quite short or much longer.","短语可以很短，也可以长得多。"],[43.25,47.08,65,"What matters is how its words work together in the sentence.","重要的是其中的词在句子里如何协同起作用。"],[47.43,54.28,65,"The major phrase categories in English include noun, prepositional, verb, and adjective phrases.","英语的主要短语类型包括名词短语、介词短语、动词短语和形容词短语。"],[55.53,59.46,66,"Let's take The bewildered tourist was lost.","以 The bewildered tourist was lost 为例。"],[59.81,63.87,66,"The phrase the bewildered tourist is built around the noun tourist.","短语 the bewildered tourist 是围绕名词 tourist 构建的。"],[64.22,66.57,66,"The other words help specify that noun.","其他词帮助限定这个名词。"],[66.92,74.1,66,"The table gives several other phrase types, such as verb, gerund, infinitive, and appositive phrases.","表中还列出了其他几种短语类型，比如动词短语、动名词短语、不定式短语和同位语短语。"],[74.45,80.02,66,"Some labels vary across grammar frameworks, so focus on recognizing the group and its role.","有些标签在不同的语法框架中有所不同，所以重点是识别出词组及其作用。"],[80.37,82.75,66,"Next we'll put those groups into a tree.","接下来，我们把这些词组放进一棵树里。"],[84.0,89.52,67,"Read the top of the tree as: a sentence expands into a noun phrase and a verb phrase.","树的顶部可以这样读：一个句子展开为一个名词短语和一个动词短语。"],[89.87,92.76,67,"We keep expanding until we reach the words.","我们不断展开，直到到达具体的词。"],[93.11,96.88,67,"This gives us a tree that shows how the pieces fit together.","这样就得到一棵树，展示了各部分如何组合在一起。"],[97.23,103.73,67,"It also helps organize how meanings combine, although syntax alone doesn't give us the full meaning.","它也有助于组织意义如何组合，尽管仅靠句法并不能得到完整的意义。"],[104.08,107.36,67,"A parser tries to recover this tree from the text.","句法分析器的任务就是从文本中恢复出这棵树。"],[108.61,112.34,68,"Parsing means working out the structure of an input.","句法分析就是弄清楚一个输入的结构。"],[112.69,117.57,68,"The diagram here uses a compiler pipeline as an analogy.","这里的图用编译器的流水线作类比。"],[117.92,125.78,68,"First the lexical analyzer identifies the tokens, then the parser organizes them into a parse tree.","先由词法分析器识别出 token，再由句法分析器把它们组织成一棵语法树。"],[126.13,127.86,68,"Natural language adds a challenge.","自然语言带来了额外的挑战。"],[128.21,133.28,68,"The same words can support more than one structure, so we need to choose among alternatives.","同样的词可以对应不止一种结构，所以我们需要在多种可能中做选择。"],[133.63,138.43,68,"Let's start with a simple example and build its tree one layer at a time.","我们从一个简单的例子开始，一层一层地构建它的树。"],[139.68,143.58,69,"Our example is The large cat eats the small rat.","我们的例子是 The large cat eats the small rat。"],[143.93,145.06,69,"Who is doing the action?","是谁在做这个动作？"],[145.41,147.36,69,"And what receives the action?","又是什么承受这个动作？"],[147.71,150.47,69,"The picture makes the event easy to imagine.","这张图让这个场景很容易想象。"],[150.82,156.19,69,"Over the next few slides, we'll show how the words build up into a structure that captures those roles.","接下来几页，我们会展示这些词如何一步步构成一个能体现这些角色的结构。"],[156.54,158.95,69,"Watch what each new layer adds.","请留意每一层新增了什么。"],[160.2,162.89,70,"For now, we just have the words in order.","现在，我们只有按顺序排列的词。"],[163.24,165.17,70,"There are no phrase nodes yet.","还没有任何短语节点。"],[165.52,169.59,70,"Before we group anything, try identifying the nouns and the verb.","在分组之前，试着找出其中的名词和动词。"],[169.94,174.7,70,"We can then add the smaller categories, such as articles and adjectives.","然后我们可以加上更小的类别，比如冠词和形容词。"],[175.05,178.9,70,"The next slide shows those word-level labels.","下一页展示了这些词级标签。"],[180.15,182.2,71,"Now each word has a category.","现在每个词都有了类别。"],[182.55,188.53,71,"The is an article, large and small are adjectives, and cat and rat are nouns.","the 是冠词，large 和 small 是形容词，cat 和 rat 是名词。"],[188.88,190.47,71,"Eats is the verb.","eats 是动词。"],[190.82,193.82,71,"These labels tell us about individual words.","这些标签告诉我们的是单个词的信息。"],[194.17,197.99,71,"They haven't yet told us which words form a larger unit.","它们还没有告诉我们哪些词构成更大的单位。"],[198.34,199.87,71,"That's the next step.","这就是下一步。"],[201.12,206.33,72,"Here we group the large cat and the small rat into two noun phrases.","这里我们把 the large cat 和 the small rat 分别组成两个名词短语。"],[206.68,211.63,72,"They have the same basic internal pattern: article, adjective, noun.","它们内部的基本模式相同：冠词、形容词、名词。"],[211.98,216.02,72,"One names the actor, and the other names the animal being eaten.","一个指的是施动者，另一个指的是被吃的动物。"],[216.37,217.95,72,"The verb is still separate.","动词仍然是单独的。"],[218.3,222.11,72,"Next we'll combine it with its object to build the predicate.","接下来，我们把它和宾语结合起来，构成谓语。"],[223.36,227.62,73,"Eats now joins the small rat under a verb phrase.","现在 eats 和 the small rat 一起归入一个动词短语。"],[227.97,231.37,73,"Together, they describe the action and its object.","它们共同描述了动作及其对象。"],[231.72,235.45,73,"The subject noun phrase stays outside that branch.","主语名词短语则留在这个分支之外。"],[235.8,241.67,73,"So the tree now separates who we're talking about from what that participant does.","这样，这棵树就把我们谈论的对象和这个参与者做了什么区分开来。"],[242.02,244.81,73,"We just need one final connection at the top.","只差顶部最后一个连接了。"],[246.06,250.93,74,"Now the noun phrase and verb phrase come together to form the sentence.","现在，名词短语和动词短语合在一起，构成了句子。"],[251.28,253.53,74,"That's our complete constituency tree.","这就是我们完整的成分句法树。"],[253.88,260.39,74,"You can follow a branch down to the individual words, or trace words upward to see which group they belong to.","你可以沿着分支向下找到具体的词，也可以从词向上追溯，看它们属于哪个词组。"],[260.74,266.3,74,"This links back to our replacement tests: many replaceable expressions correspond to a subtree.","这又回到了我们的替换测试：许多可替换的表达都对应一棵子树。"],[267.55,270.98,75,"We built this tree from the words upward.","我们是从词开始向上构建这棵树的。"],[271.33,273.02,75,"That's a bottom-up approach.","这是一种自底向上的方法。"],[273.37,277.84,75,"We could also start with a sentence node and predict the smaller groups underneath it.","我们也可以从句子节点出发，预测它下面更小的词组。"],[278.19,279.1,75,"That's top-down.","这就是自顶向下。"],[279.45,284.87,75,"Both approaches need a way to choose when several structures are possible.","两种方法都需要在多种结构都可能时做出选择。"],[285.22,290.7,75,"Before looking at ambiguity, let's try a representation that connects words directly.","在讨论歧义之前，我们先试试一种直接连接词与词的表示方法。"],[291.95,296.06,76,"Dependency syntax connects a head word to its dependents.","依存句法把中心词和它的依存词连接起来。"],[296.41,303.45,76,"In cats chase mice, chase is the main verb, and cats and mice connect to it through different relations.","在 cats chase mice 中，chase 是主要动词，cats 和 mice 通过不同的关系与它相连。"],[303.8,308.9,76,"In Tesniere's terms, the head is the governor and the dependent is the subordinate.","用 Tesnière 的术语来说，中心词叫支配词，依存词叫从属词。"],[309.25,312.0,76,"The edges have a direction and a label.","这些边既有方向，也有标签。"],[312.35,318.49,76,"That gives us a compact way to represent who does what and which words modify which.","这为我们提供了一种紧凑的方式，来表示谁做了什么、哪些词修饰哪些词。"],[318.84,320.58,76,"Let's read a larger example.","我们来读一个更大的例子。"],[321.83,326.4,77,"The sentence is Economic news had little effect on financial markets.","这个句子是 Economic news had little effect on financial markets。"],[326.75,334.14,77,"Start with the short connections: economic modifies news, little modifies effect, and financial modifies markets.","先看短的连接：economic 修饰 news，little 修饰 effect，financial 修饰 markets。"],[334.49,339.09,77,"Then follow the longer connections to see how those groups fit into the sentence.","再沿着较长的连接，看这些词组如何融入整个句子。"],[339.44,344.2,77,"News is the subject of had, and effect is its object.","news 是 had 的主语，effect 是它的宾语。"],[344.55,354.25,77,"Exact labels can vary between annotation schemes, but the goal is the same: make the word-to-word relationships explicit.","具体标签会因标注体系而异，但目标相同：把词与词之间的关系显式地表示出来。"],[355.5,358.49,78,"In a dependency tree, each word has a head.","在依存树中，每个词都有一个中心词。"],[358.84,363.34,78,"The main word connects to an artificial ROOT, which anchors the tree.","主要的词连接到一个人为设置的 ROOT 节点上，由它来锚定整棵树。"],[363.69,368.41,78,"We also rule out cycles, so a word can't eventually depend on itself.","我们还排除了环，所以一个词不会最终依存于它自己。"],[368.76,373.63,78,"Some trees have crossing edges, which brings in the idea of projectivity.","有些树的边会交叉，这就引出了投射性的概念。"],[373.98,379.59,78,"The harder problem, though, is choosing the right tree when a sentence allows several readings.","不过，更难的问题是：当一个句子有多种解读时，如何选出正确的树。"],[380.84,385.84,79,"Listen to this: One morning, I shot an elephant in my pajamas.","听听这句：One morning, I shot an elephant in my pajamas。"],[386.19,387.85,79,"Who is wearing the pajamas?","穿着睡衣的是谁？"],[388.2,391.88,79,"Now comes the punch line: How he got into my pajamas, I don't know.","笑点来了：How he got into my pajamas, I don't know。"],[392.23,395.71,79,"The joke makes us switch to the unexpected reading.","这个笑话迫使我们切换到那个出人意料的理解。"],[396.06,399.34,79,"The phrase in my pajamas can attach in two places.","in my pajamas 这个短语可以挂接在两个位置。"],[399.69,402.05,79,"Same words, different structure.","同样的词，不同的结构。"],[403.3,406.13,80,"There are several kinds of ambiguity.","歧义有好几种。"],[406.48,410.65,80,"Bank might refer to a financial institution or the side of a river.","bank 可能指银行，也可能指河岸。"],[411.0,418.0,80,"Books is a noun in The books are here and a verb in He books the flight.","books 在 The books are here 中是名词，在 He books the flight 中是动词。"],[418.35,424.46,80,"The pajamas example adds structural ambiguity, an attachment problem for the prepositional phrase.","睡衣的例子则属于结构歧义，也就是介词短语的挂接问题。"],[424.81,428.68,80,"A model often has to resolve these uncertainties together.","模型往往需要同时解决这些不确定性。"],[429.03,434.32,80,"Looking beyond one sentence can help, which takes us into discourse.","跳出单个句子去看会有帮助，这就把我们带到了篇章层面。"],[435.57,438.98,81,"Suppose someone says, She left because of that.","假设有人说：She left because of that。"],[439.33,444.68,81,"The sentence is easy to read, but we still need to know who she is and what that refers to.","这个句子很好读，但我们仍需知道 she 是谁、that 指什么。"],[445.03,447.71,81,"The answers may be in earlier sentences.","答案可能在前面的句子里。"],[448.06,452.47,81,"Discourse looks at those connections across a conversation or document.","篇章研究的就是对话或文档中句子之间的这些联系。"],[452.82,456.85,81,"This is where sentence-level analysis starts to need a wider view.","正是在这里，句子层面的分析开始需要更宽广的视野。"],[458.1,459.99,82,"Read the two sentences together.","把这两个句子放在一起读。"],[460.34,466.53,82,"The first says, this is a simple sentence, and the second says, but it is an instructive one.","第一句说 This is a simple sentence，第二句说 But it is an instructive one。"],[466.88,471.13,82,"It refers back to the sentence, and one stands in for a noun.","it 回指前面那个句子，one 则代替一个名词。"],[471.48,474.07,82,"But also tells us there's a contrast.","but 还告诉我们这里存在转折对比。"],[474.42,478.12,82,"The sentence is simple, yet it teaches us something.","这个句子很简单，却能让我们学到东西。"],[478.47,483.68,82,"To understand the flow, we need both the references and the relationship between the ideas.","要理解行文脉络，既需要弄清指代，也需要弄清观点之间的关系。"],[484.93,487.75,83,"These connections matter in real tasks.","这些联系在实际任务中很重要。"],[488.1,494.67,83,"A question-answering system may need to link a pronoun to a name several sentences earlier.","问答系统可能需要把一个代词和几句话之前出现的名字联系起来。"],[495.02,498.79,83,"A summary needs to preserve the main claims and how they relate.","摘要需要保留主要观点，以及它们之间的关系。"],[499.14,503.73,83,"Fluent individual sentences aren't enough to make a coherent document.","单个句子通顺，并不足以构成一篇连贯的文档。"],[504.08,507.75,83,"Let's look at a small example of what connects two sentences.","我们来看一个小例子，看看是什么把两个句子联系起来的。"],[509.0,511.04,84,"John hid Bill's car keys.","John 把 Bill 的车钥匙藏了起来。"],[511.39,513.21,84,"Then we learn that he was drunk.","接着我们得知，他喝醉了。"],[513.56,518.59,84,"A natural reading is that Bill was drunk, so John wanted to stop him from driving.","一种自然的理解是：Bill 喝醉了，所以 John 想阻止他开车。"],[518.94,523.47,84,"The second sentence explains the first, which is called an explanation relation.","第二句解释了第一句，这叫作解释关系。"],[523.82,529.31,84,"We used context, a pronoun interpretation, and everyday knowledge to make that connection.","我们借助上下文、对代词的理解和日常常识，建立起了这种联系。"],[529.66,533.55,84,"The spinach version feels disconnected without a larger story.","而菠菜那个版本，如果没有更大的背景故事，就显得毫无关联。"],[533.9,537.72,84,"Coherence depends on whether we can find a sensible link.","连贯与否，取决于我们能否找到合理的联系。"],[538.97,544.38,85,"In the Kansas example, the second sentence adds detail; that's elaboration.","在 Kansas 的例子中，第二句补充了细节，这是细化关系。"],[544.73,547.85,85,"In the rain example, rusting follows as a result.","在淋雨的例子中，生锈是随之而来的结果。"],[548.2,553.13,85,"The scarecrow and tin woodman examples express parallel desires.","稻草人和铁皮人的例子表达的是并列的愿望。"],[553.48,557.31,85,"These labels describe why the sentences belong together.","这些标签描述了句子为什么能放在一起。"],[557.66,564.93,85,"Different theories organize the relations in different ways, with different sets of relations and different structures.","不同的理论以不同方式组织这些关系，关系的种类和结构形式都各不相同。"],[565.28,570.51,85,"But the reading task is familiar: work out how one idea connects to the next.","但阅读任务我们都很熟悉：弄清一个想法如何连接到下一个想法。"],[571.76,576.34,86,"Sometimes the writer gives us a clear signal, such as because.","有时，作者会给出明确的信号，比如 because。"],[576.69,580.06,86,"Other times, we have to supply the connection ourselves.","另一些时候，我们得自己补上这种联系。"],[580.41,588.73,86,"In the cash example, the second sentence explains the first: high cash positions help buffer a fund when the market falls.","在现金的例子中，第二句解释了第一句：高现金头寸能在市场下跌时为基金提供缓冲。"],[589.08,593.49,86,"There's no explicit because, but readers can infer it.","这里没有明确写出 because，但读者能推断出来。"],[593.84,601.58,86,"An NLP system needs to learn these implicit links as well as recognize the obvious connecting words.","NLP 系统既要识别明显的连接词，也要学会这些隐含的联系。"],[602.83,605.67,87,"Imagine shuffling the sentences in a paragraph.","想象一下把一段话里的句子打乱顺序。"],[606.02,610.75,87,"Each sentence might still be grammatical, but the paragraph could become hard to follow.","每个句子可能仍然合乎语法，但整段话会变得难以理解。"],[611.1,617.56,87,"Pronouns might come before their referents, or an explanation might appear in the wrong place.","代词可能出现在它所指的对象之前，或者解释出现在错误的位置。"],[617.91,623.9,87,"A coherence model tries to detect that difference, scoring a document from good to bad.","连贯性模型试图识别出这种差别，给文档打出从好到差的分数。"],[624.25,629.32,87,"That helps in applications like essay scoring, summarization, and generation.","这有助于作文评分、摘要和文本生成等应用。"],[629.67,632.57,87,"We've now covered the main levels of structure.","到这里，我们已经讲完了语言结构的主要层次。"],[632.92,635.11,87,"Let's return to modern language models.","现在回到现代语言模型。"]]},{"n":5,"title":"Linguistics Meets Deep Learning: The Pendulum","blurb":"How language models replaced structured pipelines yet still encode linguistic structure implicitly, and whether the pendulum between empiricism and rationalism may swing back.","file":"lecture02_part5.mp4","poster":"lecture02_part5.jpg","duration":460.0,"first":88,"last":103,"slides":[{"n":88,"title":"Linguistic Structure in Modern NLP","t":0.8},{"n":89,"title":"Before Self-Supervised Learning","t":25.43},{"n":90,"title":"Before Self-Supervised Learning","t":55.43},{"n":91,"title":"Before Self-Supervised Learning","t":78.22},{"n":92,"title":"Language Models Solve Many Traditional NLP Tasks","t":103.1},{"n":93,"title":"How We Train Language Models Today","t":135.47},{"n":94,"title":"From Structured Pipelines to End-to-End Learning","t":159.94},{"n":95,"title":"Language Models Encode Linguistic Structure","t":185.83},{"n":96,"title":"Large Language Models Learn Linguistic Structure Implicitly","t":231.39},{"n":97,"title":"The Future Convergence of Linguistics and Deep Learning","t":262.12},{"n":98,"title":"The Pendulum Between Empiricism and Rationalism","t":284.42},{"n":99,"title":"https://languagelog.ldc.upenn.edu/myl/ldc/swung-too-far.pdf","t":311.6},{"n":100,"title":"The Empirical Era: Deep Learning's Success in NLP","t":353.18},{"n":101,"title":"The Pendulum May Swing Back","t":386.33},{"n":102,"title":"Will Linguistic Structure Return?","t":419.46},{"n":103,"title":"Break","t":443.94}],"sents":[[0.8,8.39,88,"We've seen quite a lot of structure: word forms, phrases, dependencies, and links across sentences.","我们已经看到了相当多的结构：词形、短语、依存关系，以及跨句子的联系。"],[8.74,13.97,88,"A modern language model often starts with a token sequence and a prediction task.","现代语言模型通常从一个 token 序列和一个预测任务开始。"],[14.32,17.5,88,"How does it learn to handle all these relationships?","它是如何学会处理所有这些关系的？"],[17.85,24.03,88,"Let's compare an explicit linguistic analysis with the learning approach used in large models.","我们来比较显式的语言学分析，和大模型所用的学习方法。"],[25.28,32.58,89,"Before self-supervised learning like GPT, the usual approach was to understand the human language system and try to imitate it.","在 GPT 这类自监督学习出现之前，常见的做法是先理解人类的语言系统，再去模仿它。"],[32.93,38.78,89,"Consider this review: My uncultured roommate hated this movie, but I absolutely loved it.","看这条影评：My uncultured roommate hated this movie, but I absolutely loved it。"],[39.13,42.22,89,"Is the writer's opinion positive or negative?","作者的观点是正面的还是负面的？"],[42.57,47.25,89,"A word-counting system may notice uncultured and hated and lean negative.","一个只数词的系统可能会注意到 uncultured 和 hated，于是倾向于判为负面。"],[47.6,51.15,89,"But we need to track whose opinion those words describe.","但我们需要弄清这些词描述的是谁的观点。"],[51.5,54.03,89,"Let's unpack the sentence on the next slide.","下一页我们来拆解这个句子。"],[55.28,57.18,90,"The roommate hated the movie.","室友讨厌这部电影。"],[57.53,58.67,90,"The writer loved it.","作者却很喜欢它。"],[59.02,61.72,90,"Those are two different people with different opinions.","这是两个不同的人，有着不同的观点。"],[62.07,66.72,90,"The word but sets up the contrast, and it refers back to the movie.","but 这个词引出转折，而 it 回指那部电影。"],[67.07,70.82,90,"Once we connect those pieces, the positive label makes sense.","把这些部分联系起来之后，正面的标签就说得通了。"],[71.17,76.82,90,"This example brings syntax, reference, and discourse together in one small task.","这个例子在一个小任务里同时用到了句法、指代和篇章。"],[78.07,82.72,91,"A traditional pipeline might handle those pieces in separate stages.","传统的流水线可能会分阶段处理这些部分。"],[83.07,89.11,91,"One stage builds the structure, another works out the meanings, and another connects the clauses.","一个阶段构建结构，另一个阶段确定意义，再一个阶段连接各个分句。"],[89.46,93.31,91,"We can inspect each stage, but mistakes can travel through the pipeline.","我们可以检查每个阶段，但错误会沿着流水线传递下去。"],[93.66,97.88,91,"An early wrong decision may affect the final sentiment label.","早期的一个错误决定，可能会影响最终的情感标签。"],[98.23,101.7,91,"Now compare that with the response from a language model.","现在把它和语言模型的回答比较一下。"],[102.95,106.89,92,"Here the language model gives an interpretation directly.","这里，语言模型直接给出了解读。"],[107.24,113.15,92,"It says the person who wrote the review had a positive experience, while the roommate did not enjoy the movie.","它说写影评的人看得很开心，而室友并不喜欢这部电影。"],[113.5,118.63,92,"Look at the evidence it uses: the writer says, I absolutely loved it.","看看它依据的证据：作者说 I absolutely loved it。"],[118.98,122.12,92,"This is one example, not a complete evaluation.","这只是一个例子，并不是完整的评测。"],[122.47,129.52,92,"But it shows how a shared model can handle a task that used to involve several separate components.","但它说明了一个通用模型如何处理过去需要多个独立组件才能完成的任务。"],[129.87,134.07,92,"So what in its training encourages that ability?","那么，它的训练中是什么促成了这种能力？"],[135.32,139.19,93,"A self-supervised model learns by predicting text.","自监督模型通过预测文本来学习。"],[139.54,143.53,93,"To predict a verb form, it may help to track the subject.","要预测动词的形式，跟踪主语可能会有帮助。"],[143.88,148.7,93,"To predict a continuation, it may help to remember which person we're discussing.","要预测后续内容，记住我们正在讨论哪个人可能会有帮助。"],[149.05,154.5,93,"The training task therefore rewards useful language patterns, even without a labeled syntax tree.","因此，即使没有标注好的句法树，训练任务也会奖励有用的语言规律。"],[154.85,158.54,93,"The structure can be learned indirectly through the prediction problem.","结构可以通过预测问题被间接地学到。"],[159.79,165.94,94,"Here, unstructured means the text doesn't come with every linguistic relation labeled.","这里的“非结构化”，是指文本并没有把每一种语言关系都标注出来。"],[166.29,170.0,94,"The model still has an architecture and a training objective.","模型仍然有自己的架构和训练目标。"],[170.35,176.31,94,"Also, older NLP wasn't all hand-written rules; it included statistical learning too.","另外，早期的 NLP 也并非全是手写规则，其中也包括统计学习。"],[176.66,181.56,94,"The big change is how much we learn in one shared model from large text collections.","最大的变化在于，我们能在一个通用模型里，从大规模文本中学到多少东西。"],[181.91,184.43,94,"Let's see what those representations contain.","我们来看看这些表示里到底包含了什么。"],[185.68,192.85,95,"So what do these learned representations actually contain? Researchers answer this with a tool called a probe.","那么，这些学到的表示里到底包含了什么？研究者用一种叫“探针”的工具来回答这个问题。"],[193.2,198.65,95,"A probe asks whether we can recover a linguistic label from a model's hidden states.","探针要问的是：我们能否从模型的隐藏状态中恢复出某个语言学标签。"],[199.0,203.88,95,"If we can, those states contain useful information about that label.","如果可以，说明这些隐藏状态包含了关于这个标签的有用信息。"],[204.23,213.51,95,"In this figure, sentences go into the language model, and in its latent space, subjects and objects fall along different directions.","在这张图里，句子输入语言模型，而在它的潜在空间中，主语和宾语分布在不同的方向上。"],[213.86,217.85,95,"But that doesn't prove the model uses it in every decision.","但这并不能证明模型在每一次决策中都用到了这些信息。"],[218.2,223.14,95,"We should separate information being available from information actually driving the answer.","我们应该区分“信息存在”和“信息真正驱动了答案”这两件事。"],[223.49,229.99,95,"Even with that caution, the results show that linguistic patterns can emerge without explicit supervision.","即便有这样的谨慎，结果仍然表明：语言学模式可以在没有显式监督的情况下自发出现。"],[231.24,233.87,96,"Now return to our opening picture.","现在回到我们开头的那张图。"],[234.22,240.9,96,"Before, explicit linguistic structure, like a parse tree, sat between the input and the output.","以前，显式的语言结构，比如句法树，位于输入和输出之间。"],[241.25,248.57,96,"Now, the linguistic modules are less visible, but useful structural information can still be present inside the model.","现在，语言学模块不那么显眼了，但有用的结构信息仍然可能存在于模型内部。"],[248.92,251.06,96,"That changes the questions we ask.","这改变了我们要问的问题。"],[251.41,256.71,96,"Where is the information? How can we read it out? When does it affect an answer?","信息在哪里？我们如何把它读出来？它什么时候会影响答案？"],[257.06,260.72,96,"Those questions lead naturally to the debate in the next section.","这些问题自然地引出了下一部分的争论。"],[261.97,265.31,97,"Let's step back and look at the bigger research picture.","让我们退后一步，看看更宏观的研究图景。"],[265.66,272.6,97,"The pendulum is a metaphor for changing emphasis between learning from data and building in prior structure.","钟摆是一个比喻，描述研究重心在“从数据中学习”和“内置先验结构”之间的来回变化。"],[272.95,274.45,97,"It doesn't tell us the future.","它并不能预言未来。"],[274.8,283.02,97,"It gives us a way to discuss why researchers favor different approaches at different times, and why combining them might help.","它让我们可以讨论：为什么研究者在不同时期偏爱不同的方法，以及为什么把它们结合起来可能会有帮助。"],[284.27,288.28,98,"An empirical approach learns patterns from examples.","经验主义的方法从样例中学习模式。"],[288.63,293.78,98,"A structure-driven approach builds in principles or constraints about language.","结构驱动的方法则内置关于语言的原则或约束。"],[294.13,298.24,98,"For word order, one system might learn from millions of sentences.","以语序为例，一个系统可能从数百万个句子中学习。"],[298.59,301.75,98,"Another might restrict the set of possible syntax trees.","另一个系统则可能限制可能的句法树集合。"],[302.1,304.42,98,"Many systems combine these ideas.","很多系统会把这两种思路结合起来。"],[304.77,310.2,98,"The useful question is where prior knowledge helps, and what flexibility it costs us.","真正有用的问题是：先验知识在哪里有帮助，以及它让我们付出了多少灵活性。"],[311.45,315.67,99,"This curve sketches a shift in research preferences over time.","这条曲线勾勒出研究偏好随时间的变化。"],[316.02,325.58,99,"Kenneth Church describes empiricism in the nineteen fifties, rationalism in the nineteen seventies, and empiricism again in the nineteen nineties.","Kenneth Church 描述了二十世纪五十年代的经验主义、七十年代的理性主义，以及九十年代经验主义的回归。"],[325.93,331.82,99,"And he asks whether the twenty-tens bring a return to rationalism.","他还提出疑问：二十一世纪一十年代是否会回归理性主义？"],[332.17,336.61,99,"Treat it as a conceptual picture, especially where it points toward the future.","请把它当作一张概念图，尤其是它指向未来的那一部分。"],[336.96,341.74,99,"More data and better training methods can make empirical approaches attractive.","更多的数据和更好的训练方法，会让经验主义方法更有吸引力。"],[342.09,347.92,99,"Problems with reliability or control can renew interest in explicit structure.","而可靠性或可控性方面的问题，又可能重新唤起人们对显式结构的兴趣。"],[348.27,351.78,99,"The next slides discuss both sides of that tradeoff.","接下来的几页会讨论这一权衡的两个方面。"],[353.03,358.98,100,"Today we are seeing a pronounced swing toward empiricism, driven by advances in deep learning.","今天，我们正看到一次明显的向经验主义的摆动，其动力来自深度学习的进步。"],[359.33,364.02,100,"Large-scale learning made it possible to reuse one model across many tasks.","大规模学习让同一个模型可以在许多任务中复用。"],[364.37,366.84,100,"That's a major reason for its success.","这是它成功的一个主要原因。"],[367.19,370.78,100,"The examples here don't all use the same objective.","这里提到的例子并不都使用相同的训练目标。"],[371.13,377.34,100,"BERT learns with masked prediction, while GPT-style models use autoregressive prediction.","BERT 通过掩码预测来学习，而 GPT 类模型使用自回归预测。"],[377.69,381.21,100,"Both learn from large corpora, but their direct uses differ.","两者都从大规模语料中学习，但它们的直接用途不同。"],[381.56,384.93,100,"Strong performance also leaves some open problems.","强大的性能背后，也留下了一些悬而未决的问题。"],[386.18,391.97,101,"The slide names hallucinations, lack of control, and opacity as key challenges.","这一页列出了幻觉、缺乏可控性和不透明性这几个关键挑战。"],[392.32,395.78,101,"A model can give a fluent answer that gets a fact wrong.","模型可能给出一个流畅的回答，却把事实弄错了。"],[396.13,399.17,101,"It can also ignore a required output format.","它也可能忽略要求的输出格式。"],[399.52,403.44,101,"These are situations where extra constraints may help.","在这些情况下，额外的约束可能会有帮助。"],[403.79,408.33,101,"Still, adding linguistic structure won't automatically solve every problem.","不过，加入语言结构并不会自动解决所有问题。"],[408.68,413.59,101,"The interesting direction is to combine learned capabilities with well-chosen constraints.","有意思的方向是：把学到的能力和精心选择的约束结合起来。"],[413.94,418.06,101,"Let's finish this section by considering where that combination could be useful.","最后，我们来想一想这种结合在哪些地方可能有用，以此结束这一部分。"],[419.31,422.11,102,"Where do you think explicit structure would help most?","你认为显式结构在哪里最有帮助？"],[422.46,428.47,102,"Maybe in a language with little training data, or in a task where the output must follow strict rules.","也许是在训练数据很少的语言上，或者是在输出必须遵守严格规则的任务中。"],[428.82,430.46,102,"Take a moment to think about it.","花一点时间想一想。"],[430.81,433.78,102,"And what might we lose by adding those rules?","那么，加入这些规则，我们可能会失去什么？"],[434.13,436.96,102,"Keep both the benefit and the cost in view.","要同时看到收益和代价。"],[437.31,442.54,102,"After the break, we'll look more closely at representations learned from data.","休息之后，我们会更仔细地看看从数据中学到的表示。"],[443.79,444.92,103,"Let's take a short break.","我们稍微休息一下。"],[445.27,449.65,103,"When we come back, we'll move from language structure to word representations.","回来之后，我们将从语言结构转向词的表示。"],[450.0,458.15,103,"Keep this question in mind: if we had to describe a word with a short list of numbers, what would we want those numbers to tell us?","请带着这个问题：如果要用一小串数字来描述一个词，我们希望这些数字告诉我们什么？"]]},{"n":6,"title":"Words for Search: Term Matching, TF-IDF and LSA","blurb":"Part II begins with search: term matching, the vector space model, TF-IDF weighting and latent semantic analysis with SVD as first ways to represent words.","file":"lecture02_part6.mp4","poster":"lecture02_part6.jpg","duration":446.3,"first":104,"last":124,"slides":[{"n":104,"title":"Part II: Word Representation","t":0.8},{"n":105,"title":"Application 1: Finding a Book in a Library","t":25.14},{"n":106,"title":"Application 2: Search","t":48.74},{"n":107,"title":"Application 3: Personal Assistants","t":74.4},{"n":108,"title":"For NLP","t":99.16},{"n":109,"title":"Lecture Outline","t":129.32},{"n":110,"title":"Representing Words","t":147.86},{"n":111,"title":"A Simple Example: Term Matching","t":171.25},{"n":112,"title":"Which Book Is More Relevant?","t":194.13},{"n":113,"title":"The Vector Space Model","t":219.91},{"n":114,"title":"The TF-IDF Vector Space Model","t":250.29},{"n":115,"title":"Extensions to the Vector Space Model","t":297.79},{"n":116,"title":"Latent Semantic Analysis","t":324.18},{"n":117,"title":"Latent Semantic Analysis with SVD","t":357.62},{"n":118,"title":"Beyond Exact Term Matching","t":391.82},{"n":119,"title":"Latent Semantic Analysis","t":416.54}],"sents":[[0.8,3.95,104,"Now let's begin Part Two: word representation.","现在开始第二部分：词的表示。"],[4.3,8.19,104,"In the first half, we looked at what language is made of.","在前半部分，我们看了语言是由什么构成的。"],[8.54,12.92,104,"Now we'll look at how a model represents some of that information.","现在我们来看模型如何表示其中的一些信息。"],[13.27,18.33,104,"We'll start with familiar search problems, then move from matching words to comparing vectors.","我们先从熟悉的搜索问题开始，然后从“匹配词语”过渡到“比较向量”。"],[18.68,23.74,104,"That will take us to word2vec and the idea of learning from context.","这会把我们带到 word2vec，以及从上下文中学习的思想。"],[24.99,29.6,105,"Imagine you're looking for an introductory book on artificial intelligence.","想象一下，你正在找一本人工智能的入门书。"],[29.95,36.49,105,"Several books might match the topic, but they could differ in level, language, or practical focus.","可能有好几本书都符合这个主题，但它们在难度、语言或实践侧重点上可能各不相同。"],[36.84,41.36,105,"How should the system represent your request? And how should it compare the books?","系统应该如何表示你的需求？又该如何比较这些书？"],[41.71,47.34,105,"Those questions make information retrieval a useful starting point for word representations.","正是这些问题，让信息检索成为研究词表示的一个很好的起点。"],[48.59,51.42,106,"The same issue appears in restaurant search.","同样的问题也出现在餐厅搜索中。"],[51.77,57.61,106,"You might type, somewhere to eat Indian food, while a listing says, Indian restaurant.","你可能输入“找个地方吃印度菜”，而商家的名称写的是“印度餐厅”。"],[57.96,61.39,106,"The wording differs, but the connection is clear to us.","措辞不同，但对我们来说两者的联系一目了然。"],[61.74,68.91,106,"A search system needs some way to make that connection too, while also keeping details such as the location.","搜索系统也需要某种方式建立这种联系，同时还要保留像地点这样的细节。"],[69.26,73.0,106,"Exact matching only gets us part of the way.","精确匹配只能帮我们解决一部分问题。"],[74.25,80.27,107,"Now compare, set an alarm for seven, with, wake me up at seven.","现在比较一下“设一个七点的闹钟”和“七点叫醒我”。"],[80.62,83.9,107,"In the right context, they express a similar request.","在合适的语境下，它们表达的是相似的请求。"],[84.25,88.37,107,"An assistant should connect those requests while preserving the time.","智能助手应该把这两种请求联系起来，同时保留时间信息。"],[88.72,92.02,107,"So it needs both similarity and precision.","所以它既需要相似性，也需要精确性。"],[92.37,97.76,107,"Whatever the application, the model needs useful representations of the words it receives.","无论是什么应用，模型都需要对它接收到的词有有用的表示。"],[99.01,102.31,108,"The Lego picture gives us a helpful analogy.","这张乐高的图给了我们一个很好的类比。"],[102.66,107.03,108,"Tokens are the blocks, and the model combines them into larger structures.","词元就是积木块，模型把它们组合成更大的结构。"],[107.38,111.9,108,"A good word representation makes those blocks useful across many tasks.","好的词表示能让这些积木块在许多任务中都派上用场。"],[112.25,119.09,108,"Notice the arrow: good blocks can come as a side product of the building strategy, such as language modeling.","注意这个箭头：好的积木块可以作为构建策略，比如语言建模的副产品而得到。"],[119.44,122.08,108,"The way we combine them still matters, though.","不过，我们如何组合它们仍然很重要。"],[122.43,127.92,108,"Today we'll focus on learning the representations and on what information they can capture.","今天我们重点关注如何学习这些表示，以及它们能捕捉到哪些信息。"],[129.17,135.4,109,"We'll follow two broad routes: counting patterns in text and learning vectors through a training task.","我们将沿着两条大的路线走：统计文本中的模式，以及通过训练任务学习向量。"],[135.75,138.56,109,"Both use evidence from where words occur.","两者都利用了词出现位置所提供的证据。"],[138.91,141.48,109,"We'll first see how that evidence helps search.","我们先看看这些证据如何帮助搜索。"],[141.83,146.46,109,"Then we'll use it to represent individual words and learn their relationships.","然后我们用它来表示单个的词，并学习词与词之间的关系。"],[147.71,149.99,110,"Giving each word an ID is easy.","给每个词分配一个编号很容易。"],[150.34,154.19,110,"But the ID doesn't tell us much about meaning.","但编号并不能告诉我们多少关于词义的信息。"],[154.54,158.11,110,"Suppose cat is number seven and dog is number nine hundred.","假设 cat 是七号，dog 是九百号。"],[158.46,161.37,110,"The distance between those numbers is arbitrary.","这两个数字之间的距离是任意的。"],[161.72,167.06,110,"We want a representation where useful relationships show up in the numbers themselves.","我们想要的表示，是让有用的关系直接体现在数字本身之中。"],[167.41,169.85,110,"Let's begin with a simple matching baseline.","我们先从一个简单的匹配基线开始。"],[171.1,174.13,111,"The simplest approach is to look for the same term.","最简单的方法就是寻找相同的词项。"],[174.48,178.47,111,"If the query is AI, we check whether AI appears in a title.","如果查询是 AI，我们就检查书名里是否出现了 AI。"],[178.82,180.36,111,"That can filter the collection.","这样可以对馆藏进行筛选。"],[180.71,187.1,111,"But it may miss a title that spells out artificial intelligence or uses another language.","但它可能漏掉那些把 artificial intelligence 完整拼写出来、或者使用其他语言的书名。"],[187.45,192.73,111,"And when several titles match, we still need to decide which result comes first.","而且当好几个书名都匹配时，我们仍然需要决定哪个结果排在前面。"],[193.98,197.02,112,"Suppose you want to learn AI by writing Python code.","假设你想通过写 Python 代码来学习 AI。"],[197.37,200.11,112,"A practical book may be the best choice.","一本实践型的书可能是最佳选择。"],[200.46,205.49,112,"Someone looking for a broad introduction might prefer another book.","而想要一个宽泛入门的人，可能会更喜欢另一本书。"],[205.84,210.63,112,"All the titles can match the topic, but relevance depends on the request.","所有书名都可能符合主题，但相关性取决于具体的需求。"],[210.98,215.37,112,"We need a score that can express those differences.","我们需要一个能够体现这些差异的评分。"],[215.72,218.51,112,"That's where a vector representation helps.","这正是向量表示能发挥作用的地方。"],[219.76,224.53,113,"Imagine a tiny vocabulary with just AI, Python, and cooking.","想象一个很小的词表，只有 AI、Python 和 cooking 三个词。"],[224.88,226.59,113,"Give each term a coordinate.","给每个词项分配一个坐标轴。"],[226.94,231.09,113,"Both a document and a query become three-number vectors.","这样，文档和查询都变成了由三个数构成的向量。"],[231.44,234.89,113,"Now we can compare their directions or distances.","现在我们就可以比较它们的方向或距离了。"],[235.24,241.33,113,"The score here is the cosine: the dot product of d and q, divided by the product of their lengths.","这里的评分是余弦：d 和 q 的点积，除以它们长度的乘积。"],[241.68,244.38,113,"The key is that they use the same coordinates.","关键在于它们使用相同的坐标。"],[244.73,248.89,113,"Next, we need to decide what number to put in each coordinate.","接下来，我们需要决定每个坐标上放什么数值。"],[250.14,252.92,114,"TF-IDF combines two ideas.","TF-IDF 结合了两个想法。"],[253.27,260.74,114,"A word gets more weight if it appears often in this document, but less weight if it appears in almost every document.","一个词如果在这篇文档中出现得多，权重就更高；但如果它几乎出现在每篇文档里，权重就更低。"],[261.09,266.49,114,"The final weight is simply the term frequency times the inverse document frequency.","最终的权重就是词频乘以逆文档频率。"],[266.84,269.03,114,"Suppose there are one hundred documents.","假设一共有一百篇文档。"],[269.38,273.47,114,"A word found in ten of them gets an IDF of log ten.","一个出现在其中十篇里的词，它的 IDF 就是 log 十。"],[273.82,278.29,114,"A word found in all one hundred gets zero under this formula.","而一个在全部一百篇里都出现的词，按这个公式得到的是零。"],[278.64,280.72,114,"That helps distinctive terms stand out.","这有助于让有区分度的词凸显出来。"],[281.07,285.96,114,"The log in the term frequency also limits the benefit of repeating a word.","词频里的对数也限制了重复某个词所带来的好处。"],[286.31,291.38,114,"For example, in a collection of cooking pages, recipe may be common everywhere.","举个例子，在一批烹饪网页里，recipe 这个词可能到处都有。"],[291.73,296.39,114,"Saffron might be more useful for finding the particular kind of recipe you want.","而 saffron，也就是藏红花，可能更有助于找到你想要的那类菜谱。"],[297.64,300.99,115,"We can improve both the search speed and the scoring.","我们可以同时改进搜索速度和评分方式。"],[301.34,306.74,115,"An inverted index tells us which documents contain a term, so we don't scan everything.","倒排索引告诉我们哪些文档包含某个词项，这样就不必扫描所有文档。"],[307.09,313.43,115,"Length normalization helps avoid favoring long documents just because they contain more words.","长度归一化可以避免仅仅因为长文档包含更多词就偏向它们。"],[313.78,315.89,115,"These are useful improvements.","这些都是有用的改进。"],[316.24,322.78,115,"But we still have a semantic gap: what happens when related documents use different words?","但我们仍然面临语义鸿沟：当相关的文档使用了不同的词时，该怎么办？"],[324.03,327.94,116,"Imagine one document says dog and another says canine.","想象一篇文档里写的是 dog，另一篇写的是 canine。"],[328.29,332.35,116,"Separate vocabulary dimensions don't directly connect them.","词表中彼此独立的维度并不能直接把它们联系起来。"],[332.7,337.71,116,"LSA looks for shared patterns across the collection and builds a smaller space.","LSA 在整个文档集中寻找共享的模式，并构建一个更小的空间。"],[338.06,346.57,116,"Instead of one dimension per vocabulary word, each word gets a k-dimensional vector, where k is much smaller than the vocabulary size.","不再是每个词占一个维度，而是每个词得到一个 k 维向量，其中 k 远小于词表大小。"],[346.92,352.12,116,"If those words occur in similar kinds of documents, the model can connect them.","如果这些词出现在相似类型的文档中，模型就能把它们联系起来。"],[352.47,356.22,116,"Let's look at the matrix operation that makes this possible.","我们来看看让这一切成为可能的矩阵运算。"],[357.47,361.69,117,"We start with a term-document matrix and break it into factors.","我们从一个词项-文档矩阵出发，把它分解成几个因子。"],[362.04,367.02,117,"The equation reads: X equals U times Sigma times V transpose.","这个等式读作：X 等于 U 乘以 Sigma 再乘以 V 的转置。"],[367.37,370.31,117,"Then we keep the largest k singular values.","然后我们保留最大的 k 个奇异值。"],[370.66,375.62,117,"That preserves the strongest shared patterns in a smaller space.","这样就在更小的空间里保留了最强的共享模式。"],[375.97,379.69,117,"Queries and documents must use the same projection convention.","查询和文档必须使用相同的投影方式。"],[380.04,383.61,117,"Finally, cosine similarity scores the documents.","最后，用余弦相似度给文档打分。"],[383.96,390.42,117,"You don't need every algebraic detail yet; focus on how compression can bring related patterns together.","你现在不需要掌握每一个代数细节；重点关注压缩是如何把相关的模式聚到一起的。"],[391.67,397.2,118,"Look at the patterns around rock and granite, then compare them with song and band.","看看 rock 和 granite 周围的模式，再和 song、band 比较一下。"],[397.55,401.06,118,"The shared structure helps separate different kinds of content.","这种共享结构有助于区分不同类型的内容。"],[401.41,406.98,118,"In the smaller space, a query can connect to a document even without an exact shared term.","在更小的空间里，即使没有完全相同的词，查询也能和文档联系起来。"],[407.33,412.28,118,"It won't always work, but it gives us a way to go beyond literal matching.","它并不总是奏效，但它给了我们一种超越字面匹配的方法。"],[412.63,415.14,118,"Next, let's look at its limits.","接下来，我们看看它的局限。"],[416.39,420.74,119,"LSA can connect related terms and reduce the dimension.","LSA 能把相关的词联系起来，并降低维度。"],[421.09,426.44,119,"But a good matrix approximation doesn't automatically give the best search ranking.","但一个好的矩阵近似，并不会自动带来最好的搜索排序。"],[426.79,430.91,119,"We also need to handle new documents and changes in the vocabulary.","我们还需要处理新文档以及词表的变化。"],[431.26,434.54,119,"So there are other ways to model the same text collection.","所以，对同一个文本集合还有其他的建模方式。"],[434.89,444.48,119,"Extensions such as probabilistic latent semantic analysis give the hidden structure a more explicit probabilistic interpretation.","像概率潜在语义分析这样的扩展，给隐藏结构赋予了更明确的概率解释。"]]},{"n":7,"title":"Distributional Semantics: Context Becomes Vectors","blurb":"What word meaning is, why models need it, the distributional hypothesis, co-occurrence vectors, cosine similarity and the move from sparse counts to dense vectors.","file":"lecture02_part7.mp4","poster":"lecture02_part7.jpg","duration":656.1,"first":125,"last":146,"slides":[{"n":125,"title":"Lecture Outline","t":0.8},{"n":126,"title":"Meaning as Similarity in Vector Space","t":24.75},{"n":127,"title":"How Do NLP Models Represent Words?","t":57.94},{"n":128,"title":"What Do Words Mean?","t":87.61},{"n":129,"title":"Why Represent Word Meaning in NLP Models?","t":126.24},{"n":130,"title":"Lexical resources","t":153.2},{"n":131,"title":"Lecture Outline","t":178.94},{"n":132,"title":"The Distributional Hypothesis","t":202.18},{"n":133,"title":"The Distributional Hypothesis","t":226.77},{"n":134,"title":"Inferring Meaning from Context","t":259.84},{"n":135,"title":"Inferring Meaning from Context","t":280.92},{"n":136,"title":"Comparing Contexts","t":301.92},{"n":137,"title":"Meaning from Distribution","t":323.99},{"n":138,"title":"Computing Meaning from Context","t":351.04},{"n":139,"title":"Lecture Outline","t":373.42},{"n":140,"title":"Words as Co-occurrence Vectors","t":394.57},{"n":141,"title":"Measuring Vector Similarity","t":435.17},{"n":142,"title":"Limitations of Raw Co-occurrence Counts","t":486.15},{"n":143,"title":"Sparse and Dense Vectors","t":515.96},{"n":144,"title":"Why Dense Vectors?","t":552.53},{"n":145,"title":"Count-Based Dense Vectors","t":581.37},{"n":146,"title":"Prediction-Based Dense Vectors","t":613.72}],"sents":[[0.8,6.44,125,"We've now seen vectors, similarity, and probabilistic ideas in a search setting.","到这里，我们已经在搜索场景中看到了向量、相似度和概率的思想。"],[6.79,9.45,125,"Let's bring those ideas back to individual words.","现在让我们把这些想法带回到单个的词上。"],[9.8,13.5,125,"We want a model to share information between related words.","我们希望模型能在相关的词之间共享信息。"],[13.85,19.48,125,"That way, it can learn something useful from terrible and apply some of it when it sees horrible.","这样，它从 terrible 学到的有用信息，在遇到 horrible 时也能用上一部分。"],[19.83,23.35,125,"The next few slides explain what that requires.","接下来的几页会说明这需要什么条件。"],[24.6,29.5,126,"The basic idea is simple: related words should have related vectors.","基本想法很简单：相关的词应该有相关的向量。"],[29.85,36.47,126,"Cat and dog may be close because they appear in similar contexts about pets or animals.","cat 和 dog 可能很接近，因为它们出现在关于宠物或动物的相似语境中。"],[36.82,44.1,126,"In this plot, related words such as man and woman, or king and queen, form meaningful neighborhoods.","在这张图中，相关的词，比如 man 和 woman、king 和 queen，形成了有意义的邻域。"],[44.45,49.21,126,"The individual numbers usually don't have labels like pet or animal.","其中单个的数字通常没有像“宠物”或“动物”这样的标签。"],[49.56,51.58,126,"Meaning is spread across the vector.","词义是分散在整个向量之中的。"],[51.93,56.54,126,"We'll interpret the relationships between vectors, then see how to learn them.","我们先解读向量之间的关系，再看如何学习这些向量。"],[57.79,62.98,127,"Think back to logistic regression, where features are often built by string matching.","回想一下逻辑回归，那里的特征常常是通过字符串匹配构造的。"],[63.33,66.27,127,"A string feature checks for an exact word.","字符串特征检查的是某个确切的词。"],[66.62,71.06,127,"If the feature looks for terrible, it won't activate for horrible.","如果特征找的是 terrible，那么遇到 horrible 时它不会被激活。"],[71.41,74.91,127,"A classifier then needs separate evidence for both words.","于是分类器需要分别为这两个词收集证据。"],[75.26,80.83,127,"With a useful vector representation, it can share some of that evidence.","有了有用的向量表示，它就能共享其中一部分证据。"],[81.18,86.21,127,"First, though, we need to be clear about what kind of word relationship we want to capture.","不过首先，我们需要弄清楚我们想捕捉的是哪一种词间关系。"],[87.46,90.32,128,"Couch and sofa are synonyms.","couch 和 sofa 是同义词。"],[90.67,95.71,128,"Coffee and cup are closely associated, but they don't mean the same thing.","coffee 和 cup 关联密切，但它们的意思并不相同。"],[96.06,101.49,128,"Dark and light are opposites, yet they often appear in similar contexts.","dark 和 light 是反义词，却常常出现在相似的语境中。"],[101.84,111.03,128,"Words also carry affective meanings, like valence, arousal, and dominance, as in this classic table from Osgood and colleagues.","词还带有情感意义，比如效价、唤醒度和支配度，就像 Osgood 等人这张经典表格所示。"],[111.38,113.91,128,"So similar can mean several things.","所以，“相似”可以有好几种含义。"],[114.26,119.85,128,"This matters when we train a representation, and again when we evaluate it.","这在我们训练表示时很重要，在评估表示时同样重要。"],[120.2,124.84,128,"A single similarity score doesn't explain every kind of relationship.","单一的相似度分数无法解释所有类型的关系。"],[126.09,130.09,129,"Suppose our labeled reviews contain terrible but never horrible.","假设我们带标签的评论里出现过 terrible，却从没出现过 horrible。"],[130.44,136.81,129,"If pretraining has already placed their vectors near each other, the classifier can transfer some of what it learned.","如果预训练已经让它们的向量彼此靠近，分类器就能迁移一部分学到的东西。"],[137.16,140.59,129,"That is how we can generalize to similar but unseen words.","这就是我们能够泛化到相似但未见过的词的原因。"],[140.94,144.18,129,"Here, horrible is unseen in the review training set.","这里，horrible 在评论训练集中没有出现过。"],[144.53,146.76,129,"It still needs an available embedding.","但它仍然需要有一个可用的词向量。"],[147.11,151.8,129,"That's different from a word that the embedding vocabulary can't represent at all.","这和词向量词表根本无法表示的词是两回事。"],[153.05,157.17,130,"WordNet gives us explicit lexical relationships.","WordNet 为我们提供了显式的词汇关系。"],[157.52,161.67,130,"For example, it can record that a dog is a kind of animal.","例如，它可以记录“狗是一种动物”。"],[162.02,166.74,130,"That's clear and useful, but someone has to build and maintain the resource.","这既清晰又有用，但需要有人来构建和维护这个资源。"],[167.09,170.26,130,"New words and specialized domains make that harder.","新词和专业领域会让这件事变得更难。"],[170.61,174.0,130,"Could we learn some of these relationships from ordinary text?","我们能否从普通文本中学到其中一些关系呢？"],[174.35,177.54,130,"That's the motivation for the distributional hypothesis.","这就是分布假设的动机。"],[178.79,182.21,131,"We've established why related words should share information.","我们已经说明了为什么相关的词应该共享信息。"],[182.56,186.93,131,"Now we need evidence for deciding which words are related.","现在我们需要证据来判断哪些词是相关的。"],[187.28,191.4,131,"The distributional hypothesis points us toward context.","分布假设把我们引向了上下文。"],[191.75,196.06,131,"We'll first try the idea ourselves on an unfamiliar word.","我们先在一个陌生的词上亲自试一试这个想法。"],[196.41,200.78,131,"Then we'll turn that reasoning into something a computer can calculate.","然后再把这种推理变成计算机可以计算的东西。"],[202.03,206.5,132,"The idea is that a word's use tells us something about its meaning.","其核心思想是：一个词的用法能告诉我们它的一些含义。"],[206.85,211.98,132,"If two words appear in similar environments, they may share a meaning or function.","如果两个词出现在相似的环境中，它们可能具有相同的意义或功能。"],[212.33,217.3,132,"This is a useful working hypothesis, not a complete definition of meaning.","这是一个有用的工作假设，而不是对意义的完整定义。"],[217.65,220.6,132,"Remember that opposites can share contexts too.","别忘了，反义词也可能共享上下文。"],[220.95,225.37,132,"Let's make the idea more concrete by looking at neighboring words.","我们通过观察相邻的词，把这个想法变得更具体。"],[226.62,231.57,133,"Firth's phrase is: you shall know a word by the company it keeps.","Firth 的名言是：观其伴，知其词。"],[231.92,238.3,133,"When a word appears in a text, its context is the set of words nearby, within a fixed-size window.","当一个词出现在文本中时，它的上下文就是附近固定大小窗口内的那些词。"],[238.65,242.93,133,"For banking, nearby words can tell us whether we're in a financial setting.","对于 banking 来说，附近的词能告诉我们是否处在金融语境中。"],[243.28,245.71,133,"We have to choose what context means.","我们必须选择“上下文”指的是什么。"],[246.06,250.26,133,"It might be a small window, a dependency relation, or a whole document.","它可以是一个小窗口、一个依存关系，或者整篇文档。"],[250.61,254.54,133,"That choice affects what kind of similarity the model learns.","这个选择会影响模型学到哪一种相似性。"],[254.89,258.44,133,"Now let's try a small context-reading exercise.","现在我们来做一个读上下文的小练习。"],[259.69,262.44,134,"Let's pretend we don't know the word ongchoi.","假设我们不认识 ongchoi 这个词。"],[262.79,268.06,134,"We can saute it with garlic, serve it with rice, and talk about its leaves.","它可以用蒜来炒，可以配米饭吃，人们还会谈到它的叶子。"],[268.41,270.5,134,"What kind of thing does that sound like?","这听起来像是什么东西？"],[270.85,273.69,134,"Think about it for a moment before we look at the options.","在看选项之前，先想一想。"],[274.04,275.84,134,"You already have several clues.","你已经掌握了好几条线索。"],[276.19,279.52,134,"On the next slide, we'll compare a few possible answers.","在下一页，我们会比较几个可能的答案。"],[280.77,286.43,135,"Which option fits best: a snack, a green vegetable, an alcoholic drink, or a sauce?","哪个选项最合适：零食、绿色蔬菜、酒精饮料，还是酱料？"],[286.78,288.89,135,"Leaves suggests a plant.","“叶子”暗示它是一种植物。"],[289.24,291.59,135,"The cooking phrases suggest something edible.","那些烹饪相关的说法暗示它是可以吃的。"],[291.94,295.21,135,"Taken together, the clues favor a green vegetable.","综合起来，这些线索指向绿色蔬菜。"],[295.56,300.52,135,"Now let's compare those contexts with the contexts of words we already know.","现在，我们把这些上下文和我们已经认识的词的上下文比较一下。"],[301.77,307.39,136,"Spinach and chard can also have leaves, go with rice, and be sauteed with garlic.","菠菜和莙荙菜同样有叶子，可以配米饭，也可以用蒜来炒。"],[307.74,310.99,136,"Their context patterns look similar to ongchoi's.","它们的上下文模式和 ongchoi 的很相似。"],[311.34,315.06,136,"Notice that the target words don't have to appear next to each other.","注意，这些目标词并不需要彼此相邻出现。"],[315.41,318.89,136,"They can occur in separate sentences but share similar neighbors.","它们可以出现在不同的句子里，但拥有相似的邻居词。"],[319.24,322.59,136,"That's the comparison we'll later make between vectors.","这正是我们之后要在向量之间做的比较。"],[323.84,329.16,137,"Ongchoi is a leafy green, like spinach, chard, or collard greens.","ongchoi 是一种绿叶菜，就像菠菜、莙荙菜或羽衣甘蓝一样。"],[329.51,332.41,137,"It is water spinach, also known as kangkong.","它就是空心菜，也叫 kangkong。"],[332.76,335.91,137,"We reached a useful guess before looking up a definition.","我们在查定义之前，就已经得出了一个有用的猜测。"],[336.26,338.66,137,"We haven't learned everything about the plant.","我们并没有了解这种植物的全部。"],[339.01,342.54,137,"But we have placed the word in a sensible semantic neighborhood.","但我们已经把这个词放进了一个合理的语义邻域中。"],[342.89,345.48,137,"That's already useful for an NLP model.","这对 NLP 模型来说已经很有用了。"],[345.83,349.64,137,"The next question is how to make the same comparison with numbers.","接下来的问题是：如何用数字来做同样的比较。"],[350.89,353.72,138,"For each word, count the words around it.","对每个词，统计它周围出现的词。"],[354.07,356.01,138,"That gives us a context profile.","这样就得到了一个上下文画像。"],[356.36,360.99,138,"Then compare profiles to see which words behave similarly.","然后比较这些画像，看看哪些词的行为相似。"],[361.34,366.11,138,"It's a simple idea, and we can store the profile as a vector.","这个想法很简单，而且我们可以把画像存成一个向量。"],[366.46,372.02,138,"Now we'll look at the resulting matrix and work out how to compare its rows.","现在我们来看由此得到的矩阵，并研究如何比较它的各行。"],[373.27,382.47,139,"There are three choices to follow in this section: which contexts to count, how much weight to give them, and how to reduce the size of the representation.","这一部分要关注三个选择：统计哪些上下文、给它们多大的权重，以及如何缩小表示的规模。"],[382.82,384.47,139,"We'll start with raw counts.","我们先从原始计数开始。"],[384.82,387.98,139,"Then we'll see why weighting and shorter vectors can help.","然后看看为什么加权和更短的向量会有帮助。"],[388.33,393.17,139,"This gives us the bridge from the ongchoi example to learned embeddings.","这就搭起了一座桥，从 ongchoi 的例子通向学习得到的词向量。"],[394.42,402.19,140,"Here we use word-word co-occurrence counts, with a context of four words to the left and four words to the right.","这里我们使用词-词共现计数，上下文是左边四个词加右边四个词。"],[402.54,407.83,140,"Each row represents a word, and each column represents a context word.","每一行代表一个词，每一列代表一个上下文词。"],[408.18,413.76,140,"Compare digital and information: both have strong counts around computer-related contexts.","比较 digital 和 information：两者在与计算机相关的上下文中计数都很高。"],[414.11,419.49,140,"Cherry and strawberry show a different pattern, with counts near words like pie.","cherry 和 strawberry 呈现出不同的模式，它们在 pie 这类词附近有计数。"],[419.84,425.53,140,"The vector has one coordinate per context vocabulary item, so it can be very long.","向量对上下文词表中的每一项都有一个坐标，所以它可能非常长。"],[425.88,429.56,140,"Most entries are zero, so these are sparse vectors.","大多数元素都是零，所以这些是稀疏向量。"],[429.91,433.77,140,"Before reducing it, let's decide how to measure similarity.","在压缩它之前，我们先确定如何度量相似度。"],[435.02,437.68,141,"Cosine similarity compares directions.","余弦相似度比较的是方向。"],[438.03,442.94,141,"It is the dot product of u and v, divided by the product of their lengths.","它等于 u 和 v 的点积，除以两者长度的乘积。"],[443.29,446.87,141,"Take the vectors one, one and ten, ten.","以向量 一，一 和 十，十 为例。"],[447.22,453.07,141,"One vector is much longer, but both point the same way, so their cosine is one.","其中一个向量长得多，但两者指向相同的方向，所以它们的余弦是一。"],[453.42,458.37,141,"In this plot, digital and information point in similar directions.","在这张图里，digital 和 information 指向相似的方向。"],[458.72,461.77,141,"A dot product also responds to length.","而点积还会受到长度的影响。"],[462.12,465.39,141,"That can favor frequent words with large counts.","这会偏向那些计数很大的高频词。"],[465.74,468.31,141,"Cosine removes that scale effect.","余弦消除了这种尺度效应。"],[468.66,473.89,141,"We do need special handling for a zero vector, since its norm is zero.","不过零向量需要特殊处理，因为它的范数是零。"],[474.24,478.57,141,"As a quick check, take one, zero and zero, one.","快速检验一下：取 一，零 和 零，一。"],[478.92,484.75,141,"Their dot product is zero, so their cosine is zero: they point in perpendicular directions.","它们的点积为零，所以余弦为零：它们指向互相垂直的方向。"],[486.0,487.94,142,"Raw counts have another problem.","原始计数还有另一个问题。"],[488.29,494.93,142,"The word the appears near almost everything, so a large count near cherry tells us very little.","the 这个词几乎出现在所有词旁边，所以它在 cherry 附近计数很大，也说明不了什么。"],[495.28,497.05,142,"Pie may be more informative.","pie 可能更有信息量。"],[497.4,503.6,142,"We can ask whether cherry and pie occur together more often than their separate frequencies would suggest.","我们可以问：cherry 和 pie 一起出现的次数，是否比它们各自的频率所预期的更多？"],[503.95,510.76,142,"PMI, pointwise mutual information, measures that comparison, and positive PMI clips negative values to zero.","PMI，也就是点互信息，衡量的正是这种比较；而正 PMI 会把负值截断为零。"],[511.11,514.56,142,"This gives us a better-weighted context profile.","这样我们就得到了一个加权更合理的上下文画像。"],[515.81,520.57,143,"These vectors are long because their dimension follows the vocabulary size.","这些向量很长，因为它们的维度等于词表大小。"],[520.92,527.56,143,"Sparse storage saves memory, but related context words still occupy separate coordinates.","稀疏存储能节省内存，但相关的上下文词仍然占据着各自独立的坐标。"],[527.91,534.78,143,"Classic word embeddings often use just fifty to three hundred dimensions, with dense, real-valued entries.","经典的词向量通常只用五十到三百维，并且元素是稠密的实数值。"],[535.13,540.05,143,"That's a common range for these static representations, not a rule for every modern model.","这是这类静态表示的常见范围，并不是所有现代模型都要遵守的规则。"],[540.4,546.0,143,"Short, dense vectors like these are the basis for modern NLP systems.","像这样短而稠密的向量，是现代 NLP 系统的基础。"],[546.35,551.13,143,"Let's see what we gain by moving to a smaller space.","我们来看看转到更小的空间能带来什么好处。"],[552.38,556.66,144,"Short vectors are easier to use as features in machine learning systems.","短向量更容易作为特征用在机器学习系统中。"],[557.01,561.41,144,"Suppose one word often appears with car and another with automobile.","假设一个词常和 car 一起出现，另一个词常和 automobile 一起出现。"],[561.76,564.83,144,"Raw counts put those observations in different columns.","原始计数会把这些观测放在不同的列里。"],[565.18,572.83,144,"A smaller shared representation can connect car and automobile, which can also bring the two target words closer.","一个更小的共享表示可以把 car 和 automobile 联系起来，从而也让这两个目标词彼此靠近。"],[573.18,575.4,144,"That's one route to generalization.","这是通向泛化的一条途径。"],[575.75,579.97,144,"Whether it helps our application is something we still have to test.","至于它是否对我们的应用有帮助，仍然需要实际测试。"],[581.22,586.59,145,"One way to get short vectors is SVD, which we saw earlier with LSA.","获得短向量的一种方法是 SVD，我们之前在 LSA 中见过它。"],[586.94,592.63,145,"Here we apply it to a word-context matrix, often after PPMI weighting.","这里我们把它用在词-上下文矩阵上，通常会先做 PPMI 加权。"],[592.98,596.9,145,"We keep k important directions, for example one hundred.","我们保留 k 个重要的方向，比如一百个。"],[597.25,602.21,145,"These are combinations of patterns across the matrix, rather than just k selected words.","这些方向是整个矩阵中模式的组合，而不只是挑选出来的 k 个词。"],[602.56,606.77,145,"Each row of W then serves as the embedding for one word.","于是 W 的每一行就作为一个词的词向量。"],[607.12,611.22,145,"Another approach learns the vectors through a prediction task.","另一种方法是通过预测任务来学习这些向量。"],[611.57,612.32,145,"That's next.","这就是我们接下来要讲的。"],[613.57,619.95,146,"Instead of building and decomposing the whole matrix, we can train a model to predict useful patterns.","我们不必构建并分解整个矩阵，而是可以训练一个模型来预测有用的模式。"],[620.3,625.89,146,"For example, a classifier predicts whether pie is likely to appear in the context of cherry.","例如，让一个分类器预测 pie 是否可能出现在 cherry 的上下文中。"],[626.24,632.62,146,"Word2vec learns from local context, and FastText adds information from subword pieces.","word2vec 从局部上下文中学习，FastText 则额外加入了子词片段的信息。"],[632.97,638.52,146,"GloVe is a related method with a different objective: it fits global co-occurrence statistics.","GloVe 是一种相关的方法，但目标不同：它拟合的是全局共现统计。"],[638.87,644.12,146,"Baroni and colleagues compared the two families in a paper titled, Don't count, predict!","Baroni 等人在一篇题为《Don't count, predict!》的论文中比较了这两类方法。"],[644.47,647.88,146,"The boundaries between these categories aren't perfectly sharp.","这些类别之间的界限并不是泾渭分明的。"],[648.23,654.26,146,"What they share is the goal of learning useful dense vectors, also called word embeddings.","它们的共同目标是学习有用的稠密向量，也就是所谓的词嵌入。"]]},{"n":8,"title":"Word Embeddings and the Word2vec Skip-Gram Model","blurb":"What learned word embeddings capture, including analogies, cross-lingual structure, semantic change and bias, then the skip-gram objective, its softmax and its parameters.","file":"lecture02_part8.mp4","poster":"lecture02_part8.jpg","duration":560.9,"first":147,"last":163,"slides":[{"n":147,"title":"Lecture Outline","t":0.8},{"n":148,"title":"Word Embedding Approaches","t":19.96},{"n":149,"title":"The Word Embedding Learning Problem","t":52.56},{"n":150,"title":"Word Similarity in Embedding Space","t":80.61},{"n":151,"title":"Relational Structure in Word Embeddings","t":120.5},{"n":152,"title":"Cross-Lingual Structure in Word Embeddings","t":151.13},{"n":153,"title":"Embeddings and Historical Semantic Change","t":176.33},{"n":154,"title":"Cultural Bias in Word Embeddings","t":210.8},{"n":155,"title":"Word2vec","t":252.83},{"n":156,"title":"The Skip-Gram Model","t":282.03},{"n":157,"title":"Skip-Gram Training Pairs","t":311.58},{"n":158,"title":"The Skip-Gram Objective","t":335.92},{"n":159,"title":"Defining the Context Probability","t":390.32},{"n":160,"title":"Why the Objective Produces Useful Embeddings","t":433.13},{"n":161,"title":"How Many Parameters Does the Model Have?","t":464.37},{"n":162,"title":"How Many Parameters Does the Model Have?","t":492.78},{"n":163,"title":"The Two Word2vec Embedding Matrices","t":518.69}],"sents":[[0.8,3.81,147,"We now have the motivation for dense word vectors.","现在我们已经有了使用稠密词向量的动机。"],[4.16,7.33,147,"Let's make the learning process concrete with word2vec.","我们用 word2vec 把学习过程具体化。"],[7.68,13.74,147,"We'll define a small prediction task, write down its objective, and see which parameters it learns.","我们将定义一个小的预测任务，写出它的目标函数，并看看它学习了哪些参数。"],[14.09,18.56,147,"Then we'll address the cost of training it on a large vocabulary.","然后再讨论在大词表上训练它的代价。"],[19.81,24.24,148,"Our goal is to represent words as short, dense vectors.","我们的目标是把词表示成短而稠密的向量。"],[24.59,32.27,148,"The left side starts from an explicit co-occurrence matrix, often PPMI weighted and decomposed with SVD.","左边从一个显式的共现矩阵出发，通常经过 PPMI 加权，再用 SVD 分解。"],[32.62,36.8,148,"The right side emphasizes learning parameters through an objective.","右边强调的是通过一个目标函数来学习参数。"],[37.15,40.51,148,"GloVe connects these views by learning from global counts.","GloVe 通过从全局计数中学习，把这两种视角联系了起来。"],[40.86,44.12,148,"All these approaches use distributional evidence.","所有这些方法都利用了分布式的证据。"],[44.47,51.16,148,"The next slide gives us the basic input and output, before we look at what the learned space can do.","下一页会给出基本的输入和输出，然后我们再看学到的空间能做些什么。"],[52.41,56.58,149,"We provide a corpus, a vocabulary, and a dimension d.","我们提供一个语料库、一个词表和一个维度 d。"],[56.93,59.72,149,"The output is one d-dimensional vector per word.","输出是每个词对应的一个 d 维向量。"],[60.07,63.6,149,"If d is three hundred, that's three hundred numbers.","如果 d 等于三百，那就是三百个数字。"],[63.95,67.56,149,"We usually can't assign a fixed meaning to each coordinate.","我们通常无法给每个坐标赋予固定的含义。"],[67.91,72.68,149,"For example, rotating all vectors together preserves their dot products.","例如，把所有向量一起旋转，它们的点积保持不变。"],[73.03,79.21,149,"The useful information is mainly in how the vectors relate to one another.","有用的信息主要体现在向量彼此之间的关系上。"],[80.46,83.07,150,"Here we're looking for words close to Sweden.","这里我们在寻找与 Sweden 接近的词。"],[83.42,88.59,150,"We look for the word whose embedding has the highest cosine with Sweden's embedding.","我们寻找那个与 Sweden 的词向量余弦值最高的词。"],[88.94,97.47,150,"Other countries, like Norway, Denmark, and Finland, are reasonable neighbors because they often occur in related contexts.","其他国家，比如挪威、丹麦和芬兰，是合理的近邻，因为它们常常出现在相关的上下文中。"],[97.82,102.51,150,"We exclude Sweden itself, otherwise the nearest match is trivial.","我们排除 Sweden 本身，否则最近的匹配就毫无意义了。"],[102.86,109.62,150,"Also, the screenshot says distance, but these descending values are being used as similarity scores.","另外，截图里写的是“distance”，但这些递减的数值实际上是被当作相似度分数来用的。"],[109.97,115.24,150,"Higher cosine means a closer directional match, and cosine ranges from minus one to one.","余弦越高，方向越接近；余弦的取值范围是负一到一。"],[115.59,119.1,150,"Next we'll look at relationships beyond simple neighbors.","接下来我们看看超越简单近邻的关系。"],[120.35,125.53,151,"The familiar example starts with king, subtracts man, and adds woman.","那个熟悉的例子是：从 king 出发，减去 man，再加上 woman。"],[125.88,130.51,151,"We then look for a nearby vector, often queen in a suitable model.","然后寻找附近的向量，在合适的模型里通常就是 queen。"],[130.86,135.3,151,"The idea is that a relationship can show up as a direction in the space.","其思想是：一种关系可以表现为空间中的一个方向。"],[135.65,140.77,151,"The same kind of pattern appears for verb tense, and for countries and their capitals.","同样的模式也出现在动词时态上，以及国家和首都之间。"],[141.12,144.14,151,"It's an approximate pattern, not a guaranteed rule.","这是一种近似的模式，而不是一定成立的规则。"],[144.49,149.73,151,"The next slide asks whether related geometry can also appear across languages.","下一页要问的是：类似的几何结构是否也会跨语言出现。"],[150.98,157.93,152,"The Spanish and English spaces may have similar patterns, but their coordinates won't automatically line up.","西班牙语和英语的向量空间可能有相似的模式，但它们的坐标并不会自动对齐。"],[158.28,161.27,152,"The matrix W learns a mapping between them.","矩阵 W 学习的就是它们之间的映射。"],[161.62,164.86,152,"After that mapping, cuatro can be close to four.","经过这个映射之后，cuatro 就可以和 four 靠得很近。"],[165.21,169.2,152,"The number and animal examples help us visualize the idea.","数字和动物的例子帮助我们直观地理解这个想法。"],[169.55,174.93,152,"Translation still needs context, but shared geometry gives us a useful starting point.","翻译仍然需要上下文，但共享的几何结构给了我们一个有用的起点。"],[176.18,182.24,153,"We can also train vectors on text from different decades and compare a word's neighbors over time.","我们还可以在不同年代的文本上训练向量，比较一个词的近邻随时间的变化。"],[182.59,191.6,153,"Here, gay drifts away from cheerful, broadcast moves from sowing seeds to radio and television, and awful moves from majestic to terrible.","这里，gay 逐渐远离了 cheerful，broadcast 从“撒种”变成了广播和电视，awful 则从“庄严”变成了“糟糕”。"],[191.95,194.82,153,"That gives us clues about changing usage.","这为我们提供了用法变化的线索。"],[195.17,198.88,153,"We need to align the spaces and check the corpora carefully.","我们需要对齐这些空间，并仔细检查语料。"],[199.23,201.71,153,"A change in genre can also move a vector.","文体的变化也可能让向量发生移动。"],[202.06,209.4,153,"So a neighborhood shift is something to investigate, rather than a complete historical explanation on its own.","所以，邻域的变化是一个值得研究的线索，而不是它本身就构成完整的历史解释。"],[210.65,214.07,154,"The same learning process can pick up social stereotypes.","同样的学习过程也会习得社会刻板印象。"],[214.42,218.94,154,"Paris is to France as Tokyo is to Japan; that analogy works well.","巴黎之于法国，就像东京之于日本；这个类比效果很好。"],[219.29,227.7,154,"But father is to doctor as mother is to nurse, and man is to computer programmer as woman is to homemaker.","但是，父亲之于医生，被类比成母亲之于护士；男人之于程序员，被类比成女人之于家庭主妇。"],[228.05,232.76,154,"In these examples, occupational relationships become associated with gender.","在这些例子中，职业关系与性别关联在了一起。"],[233.11,237.37,154,"Those are patterns in the data, not statements about people's abilities.","这些是数据中的模式，而不是对人的能力的陈述。"],[237.72,244.67,154,"If we use the vectors in a hiring or recommendation system, the associations can affect real decisions.","如果我们把这些向量用在招聘或推荐系统里，这些关联就可能影响真实的决策。"],[245.02,251.43,154,"That gives us another reason to inspect embeddings carefully, beyond checking their average accuracy.","这又给了我们一个理由：除了检查平均准确率之外，还要仔细审视词向量。"],[252.68,258.52,155,"Word2vec was introduced by Tomas Mikolov and colleagues in two thousand thirteen.","word2vec 由 Tomas Mikolov 等人在二零一三年提出。"],[258.87,260.71,155,"It has two classic setups.","它有两种经典的设定。"],[261.06,266.48,155,"CBOW, the continuous bag of words, uses the surrounding words to predict the middle word.","CBOW，即连续词袋模型，用周围的词来预测中间的词。"],[266.83,271.18,155,"Skip-gram uses the middle word to predict its neighbors.","Skip-gram 则用中间的词来预测它的邻居。"],[271.53,273.42,155,"Look at the direction of the arrows.","看看箭头的方向。"],[273.77,276.15,155,"That's the easiest way to remember the difference.","这是记住两者区别最简单的方法。"],[276.5,280.63,155,"We'll follow Skip-gram first, starting with one window of text.","我们先跟着 Skip-gram 走，从一个文本窗口开始。"],[281.88,286.19,156,"The key idea is to use each word to predict other words in its context.","核心思想是：用每个词去预测它上下文中的其他词。"],[286.54,290.36,156,"Choose a center word and take up to m words on each side.","选一个中心词，在它两边各取最多 m 个词。"],[290.71,294.75,156,"Here m is two, so we have up to four context words.","这里 m 等于二，所以最多有四个上下文词。"],[295.1,301.11,156,"For each center, the model gives a probability distribution over possible neighbors.","对每个中心词，模型给出一个关于可能邻居的概率分布。"],[301.46,303.63,156,"So this is a classification problem!","所以这是一个分类问题！"],[303.98,306.21,156,"The probabilities must add up to one.","这些概率加起来必须等于一。"],[306.56,310.18,156,"Let's now move the window and see the training pairs it creates.","现在我们移动窗口，看看它产生了哪些训练样本对。"],[311.43,317.42,157,"With into at the center, we get pairs with problems, turning, banking, and crises.","以 into 为中心词时，我们得到它与 problems、turning、banking 和 crises 组成的词对。"],[317.77,322.3,157,"Move the center to banking, and we get a new set of pairs.","把中心移到 banking，我们又得到一组新的词对。"],[322.65,325.54,157,"That's how ordinary text becomes training data.","普通的文本就是这样变成训练数据的。"],[325.89,329.61,157,"We want the observed pairs to receive high probabilities.","我们希望观察到的词对得到较高的概率。"],[329.96,334.52,157,"The next equation just collects that goal across all the windows in the corpus.","下一个公式只是把这个目标汇总到语料中的所有窗口上。"],[335.77,342.78,158,"The likelihood multiplies, over every position, the probability of each context word given the center word.","似然函数在每个位置上，把给定中心词时各个上下文词的概率连乘起来。"],[343.13,346.36,158,"Theta stands for all the parameters to be optimized.","theta 代表所有待优化的参数。"],[346.71,350.37,158,"In the loss, the outer sum goes through center positions.","在损失函数中，外层求和遍历所有中心位置。"],[350.72,353.62,158,"The inner sum goes through nearby offsets.","内层求和遍历附近的偏移量。"],[353.97,358.51,158,"We skip zero, because that's the center occurrence itself.","我们跳过零，因为那是中心词本身。"],[358.86,361.83,158,"We minimize the average negative log likelihood.","我们最小化平均负对数似然。"],[362.18,366.1,158,"If a real neighbor gets a tiny probability, the penalty is large.","如果一个真实的邻居只得到很小的概率，惩罚就会很大。"],[366.45,371.0,158,"Logs also let us add terms instead of multiplying lots of small numbers.","取对数还让我们可以把各项相加，而不必连乘许多很小的数。"],[371.35,378.88,158,"For example, if a true neighbor gets probability zero point eight, its negative natural log is about zero point two two.","例如，如果一个真实邻居的概率是零点八，它的负自然对数约为零点二二。"],[379.23,385.8,158,"At probability zero point one, the loss is about two point three, so a confident mistake costs much more.","如果概率是零点一，损失大约是二点三，所以自信的错误代价要高得多。"],[386.15,388.92,158,"Now we need to define those probabilities.","现在我们需要定义这些概率。"],[390.17,395.61,159,"Each word has two vectors: u for its center role and v for its context role.","每个词有两个向量：u 用于它作为中心词的角色，v 用于它作为上下文词的角色。"],[395.96,398.1,159,"Their dot product gives a score.","它们的点积给出一个分数。"],[398.45,401.69,159,"Softmax turns the scores into probabilities.","softmax 把这些分数变成概率。"],[402.04,407.72,159,"We exponentiate each score, then divide by the sum over all possible context words.","我们对每个分数取指数，然后除以所有可能上下文词的指数和。"],[408.07,412.46,159,"A stronger score relative to the others means a higher probability.","相对于其他词分数越高，概率就越高。"],[412.81,422.47,159,"For three candidate scores of two, one, and zero, softmax gives about zero point six seven, zero point two four, and zero point zero nine.","对于二、一、零这三个候选分数，softmax 给出的概率约为零点六七、零点二四和零点零九。"],[422.82,426.74,159,"They add up to one, and the largest score gets the largest share.","它们加起来等于一，分数最大的那个得到最大的份额。"],[427.09,431.73,159,"That sum over the vocabulary will soon become our main cost.","这个对整个词表的求和，很快就会成为我们的主要计算代价。"],[432.98,435.48,160,"Why does this produce useful word vectors?","为什么这样能得到有用的词向量？"],[435.83,437.33,160,"Think about cat and dog.","想想 cat 和 dog。"],[437.68,444.47,160,"If they share many context patterns, the model is encouraged to give them similar behavior when predicting neighbors.","如果它们共享许多上下文模式，模型在预测邻居时就会倾向于让它们表现得相似。"],[444.82,446.98,160,"No one labels a dimension as pet.","没有人把某一维标注为“宠物”。"],[447.33,451.55,160,"The relationship develops through repeated prediction tasks.","这种关系是在反复的预测任务中逐渐形成的。"],[451.9,459.86,160,"After training, we reuse the learned vectors, and with a large enough corpus they become very good word representations.","训练之后，我们复用学到的向量；只要语料足够大，它们就会成为非常好的词表示。"],[460.21,462.97,160,"Let's check how many parameters that involves.","我们来算一算这涉及多少参数。"],[464.22,466.14,161,"How many parameters do we have?","我们一共有多少参数？"],[466.49,469.09,161,"Think of the two embedding tables.","想一想那两张词向量表。"],[469.44,474.1,161,"How many rows are in each table, and how many numbers are in each row?","每张表有多少行，每一行又有多少个数？"],[474.45,481.78,161,"The options are d times the vocabulary size, two d times it, two m times it, or two m d times it.","选项分别是：d 乘以词表大小、二 d 乘以词表大小、二 m 乘以词表大小，或者二 m d 乘以词表大小。"],[482.13,488.62,161,"Also ask yourself whether a larger context window creates new parameters, or simply more training pairs.","也问问自己：更大的上下文窗口是会产生新的参数，还是只会产生更多的训练样本对？"],[488.97,491.38,161,"We'll check the count on the next slide.","我们在下一页核对答案。"],[492.63,496.63,162,"The answer is b: two times the vocabulary size times d.","答案是 b：二乘以词表大小再乘以 d。"],[496.98,499.53,162,"Each word has two d-dimensional vectors.","每个词有两个 d 维向量。"],[499.88,509.21,162,"For fifty thousand words and three hundred dimensions, that's thirty million scalar parameters, before optimizer state or other overhead.","对于五万个词、三百维来说，就是三千万个标量参数，这还不算优化器状态或其他开销。"],[509.56,513.79,162,"A larger window creates more training pairs, but the tables stay the same size.","更大的窗口会产生更多训练样本对，但两张表的大小保持不变。"],[514.14,517.29,162,"Next, let's discuss why there are two tables.","接下来，我们讨论为什么要有两张表。"],[518.54,522.62,163,"A word plays two roles here: center and context.","在这里，一个词扮演两种角色：中心词和上下文词。"],[522.97,526.45,163,"Separate vectors let the model score those roles more flexibly.","分开的向量让模型可以更灵活地为这两种角色打分。"],[526.8,535.01,163,"The slide's intuition is that a word is unlikely to appear in its own context window, so P of dog given dog should be low.","这一页给出的直觉是：一个词不太可能出现在自己的上下文窗口里，所以给定 dog 时 dog 的概率应该很低。"],[535.36,542.43,163,"With one shared vector, a word's self-score would be its squared norm, which is an extra constraint.","如果只用一个共享向量，一个词与自身的分数就是它范数的平方，这是一个额外的约束。"],[542.78,547.64,163,"Words can occur near themselves, so this example is only an intuition.","词其实也可能出现在自己附近，所以这个例子只是一种直觉。"],[547.99,551.62,163,"After training, which vectors do we use as word embeddings?","训练之后，我们用哪一组向量作为词向量？"],[551.97,559.08,163,"Choosing u, combining u and v, or using another option is an empirical decision.","选用 u、把 u 和 v 结合起来，还是采用别的方案，这是一个经验性的决定。"]]},{"n":9,"title":"Negative Sampling, GloVe and Evaluating Embeddings","blurb":"Making word2vec cheap with negative sampling, comparing CBOW and GloVe, using pretrained embeddings, and evaluating vectors extrinsically and with similarity and analogy tests.","file":"lecture02_part9.mp4","poster":"lecture02_part9.jpg","duration":520.1,"first":164,"last":180,"slides":[{"n":164,"title":"Lecture Outline","t":0.8},{"n":165,"title":"Lecture Outline","t":21.52},{"n":166,"title":"Skip-Gram with Negative Sampling","t":44.13},{"n":167,"title":"Skip-Gram with Negative Sampling","t":84.77},{"n":168,"title":"Understanding SGNS","t":134.28},{"n":169,"title":"Understanding SGNS","t":165.32},{"n":170,"title":"Continuous Bag of Words","t":197.08},{"n":171,"title":"GloVe: Global Vectors","t":224.14},{"n":172,"title":"Available Pretrained Word Embeddings","t":255.54},{"n":173,"title":"Using Pretrained Word Embeddings","t":283.67},{"n":174,"title":"Lecture Outline","t":317.23},{"n":175,"title":"Extrinsic and Intrinsic Evaluation","t":336.58},{"n":176,"title":"Extrinsic Evaluation","t":363.32},{"n":177,"title":"Intrinsic Evaluation: Word Similarity","t":395.23},{"n":178,"title":"Intrinsic Evaluation: Word Similarity","t":427.5},{"n":179,"title":"Intrinsic Evaluation: Word Analogy","t":454.04},{"n":180,"title":"Intrinsic Evaluation: Word Analogy","t":485.83}],"sents":[[0.8,4.15,164,"The full gradient derivation is in the appendix.","完整的梯度推导放在附录中。"],[4.5,10.24,164,"For now, keep one cost in mind: each training pair uses a softmax over the whole vocabulary.","现在只需记住一项代价：每个训练样本对都要在整个词表上计算一次 softmax。"],[10.59,14.03,164,"With hundreds of thousands of words, that adds up quickly.","当词表有几十万个词时，这个开销会迅速累积。"],[14.38,20.12,164,"We'll first see how negative sampling makes training cheaper, then compare a few related embedding methods.","我们先看负采样如何让训练更便宜，再比较几种相关的词向量方法。"],[21.37,27.89,165,"We observed one center-context pair, but full softmax compares it with every possible context word.","我们只观察到一个中心词-上下文词对，但完整的 softmax 要把它和每一个可能的上下文词进行比较。"],[28.24,29.19,165,"That's expensive.","这代价很高。"],[29.54,32.44,165,"Can we learn from a much smaller set of comparisons?","我们能否从少得多的比较中学习？"],[32.79,38.38,165,"Negative sampling does that by turning the task into a series of binary decisions.","负采样正是这样做的：它把任务变成一系列二元判断。"],[38.73,42.73,165,"Let's see what counts as a positive and a negative example.","我们来看看什么算正例，什么算负例。"],[43.98,51.89,166,"Here is the problem: for every pair, the full softmax gradient touches the context vectors of every word in the vocabulary.","问题在于：对于每一个词对，完整 softmax 的梯度都会涉及词表中每个词的上下文向量。"],[52.24,55.1,166,"Suppose apricot and jam appear together.","假设 apricot 和 jam 一起出现。"],[55.45,56.76,166,"That's a positive pair.","这就是一个正例词对。"],[57.11,62.72,166,"We then sample a few noise words, usually five to twenty, to create negative pairs.","然后我们采样几个噪声词，通常是五到二十个，来构造负例词对。"],[63.07,67.75,166,"We train the model to distinguish the observed pair from those samples.","我们训练模型去区分观察到的词对和这些采样出来的词对。"],[68.1,71.5,166,"Each score passes through the sigmoid function.","每个分数都会经过 sigmoid 函数。"],[71.85,78.69,166,"This is a different objective from full softmax, rather than an exact shortcut for its probabilities.","这是一个不同于完整 softmax 的目标函数，而不是计算其概率的精确捷径。"],[79.04,83.37,166,"The goal is to learn useful vectors with much less work per pair.","目标是在每个词对上花少得多的计算，学到有用的向量。"],[84.62,90.62,167,"The key idea is to convert the V-way classification into a set of binary classification tasks.","核心思想是把 V 路分类转化为一组二分类任务。"],[90.97,95.66,167,"The observed pair gets label one, and the sampled pairs get label zero.","观察到的词对标签为一，采样出的词对标签为零。"],[96.01,100.55,167,"A sigmoid turns each dot-product score into a binary probability.","sigmoid 把每个点积分数变成一个二元概率。"],[100.9,104.65,167,"A negative sample doesn't mean the words could never occur together.","负样本并不意味着这两个词永远不会一起出现。"],[105.0,107.49,167,"It's a sampled contrast for this update.","它只是这次更新中采样出来的对照。"],[107.84,115.02,167,"A common sampling distribution uses word frequency raised to the three-quarter power, then normalizes it.","一种常见的采样分布是把词频取四分之三次方，然后再归一化。"],[115.37,123.12,167,"For intuition, a score of zero gives a sigmoid value of zero point five, and a score of two gives about zero point eight eight.","直观地说，分数为零时 sigmoid 值是零点五，分数为二时大约是零点八八。"],[123.47,129.49,167,"The positive example pushes its score upward, and a negative example pushes its score downward.","正例会把它的分数往上推，负例则把它的分数往下压。"],[129.84,132.88,167,"Now let's count how many vectors we touch.","现在我们来数一数涉及了多少个向量。"],[134.13,139.21,168,"Here is the SGNS loss for one positive pair and K negative samples.","这是一个正例词对加 K 个负样本时的 SGNS 损失。"],[139.56,142.19,168,"Which embedding rows appear in this loss?","这个损失中出现了哪些词向量行？"],[142.54,148.95,168,"The options are K d, two K d, K plus one times d, or K plus two times d.","选项分别是：K 乘 d、二 K 乘 d、K 加一乘 d，或者 K 加二乘 d。"],[149.3,150.65,168,"Take a moment to decide.","花点时间做出你的判断。"],[151.0,155.01,168,"Remember that all the comparisons share the same center word.","记住，所有这些比较都共享同一个中心词。"],[155.36,158.96,168,"Count that vector only once, then count the context vectors.","这个向量只算一次，然后再数上下文向量。"],[159.31,163.92,168,"We're asking about one update, not all the parameters stored by the model.","我们问的是一次更新，而不是模型存储的全部参数。"],[165.17,169.2,169,"The answer is d: K plus two, times d.","答案是 d：K 加二，再乘以 d。"],[169.55,175.22,169,"We touch one center vector, one positive context vector, and K negative context vectors.","我们涉及一个中心向量、一个正例上下文向量，以及 K 个负例上下文向量。"],[175.57,179.56,169,"That's K plus two vectors, each with d entries.","一共是 K 加二个向量，每个有 d 个元素。"],[179.91,187.74,169,"For K equal to five and d equal to three hundred, that's two thousand one hundred entries under the usual distinct-sample count.","当 K 等于五、d 等于三百时，按通常互不重复的样本计算，就是两千一百个元素。"],[188.09,191.4,169,"Repeated samples can reduce the number of distinct rows.","如果采样有重复，不同的行数会更少。"],[191.75,195.68,169,"Either way, we avoid updating the full context table.","无论哪种情况，我们都避免了更新整张上下文向量表。"],[196.93,200.44,170,"CBOW reverses the prediction direction.","CBOW 把预测的方向反了过来。"],[200.79,206.82,170,"For the sentence, the cat sat on the mat, we might use the surrounding words to predict sat.","对于句子 the cat sat on the mat，我们可以用周围的词来预测 sat。"],[207.17,211.45,170,"The basic model combines the context vectors, often by averaging them.","基本模型会把上下文向量组合起来，通常是取平均。"],[211.8,216.0,170,"That loses their order, but gives us a simple predictor.","这会丢失词序，但给了我们一个简单的预测器。"],[216.35,218.6,170,"Compare the arrows with Skip-gram.","把这里的箭头和 Skip-gram 的比较一下。"],[218.95,222.74,170,"Next, GloVe will use global counts more directly.","接下来，GloVe 会更直接地利用全局计数。"],[223.99,228.73,171,"GloVe tries to make a vector score match the log of a co-occurrence count.","GloVe 试图让向量的分数去拟合共现次数的对数。"],[229.08,235.55,171,"Bias terms account for some overall frequency effects, and the weighting function controls how much each pair matters.","偏置项解释了一部分整体频率效应，而权重函数控制每个词对的重要程度。"],[235.9,240.87,171,"We normally train on nonzero co-occurrences because log zero is undefined.","我们通常只在非零的共现上训练，因为 log 零没有定义。"],[241.22,246.19,171,"The slide also notes that it trains faster and scales to very large corpora.","这一页还指出，它训练更快，并能扩展到非常大的语料。"],[246.54,254.14,171,"So GloVe learns from global count information, rather than using exactly the same local objective as Skip-gram.","所以 GloVe 是从全局计数信息中学习，而不是使用和 Skip-gram 完全相同的局部目标。"],[255.39,258.84,172,"You don't always have to train embeddings yourself.","你并不总是需要自己训练词向量。"],[259.19,265.45,172,"Pretrained files are available for word2vec, GloVe, and FastText, but check what data they came from.","word2vec、GloVe 和 FastText 都有现成的预训练文件，但要查清它们来自什么数据。"],[265.8,271.83,172,"News text and social media can give you different neighborhoods, even with the same vector dimension.","即使向量维度相同，新闻文本和社交媒体也可能给出不同的近邻。"],[272.18,275.8,172,"Also check case handling and vocabulary coverage.","还要检查大小写的处理方式和词表覆盖率。"],[276.15,279.08,172,"Choose a model that fits your task, then test it.","选择一个适合你任务的模型，然后对它进行测试。"],[279.43,282.27,172,"The next slide shows a basic way to use one.","下一页展示了一种基本的使用方式。"],[283.52,289.45,173,"The basic workflow is simple: load the vectors, look up a word, and compare it with other words.","基本流程很简单：加载向量，查找一个词，再把它和其他词进行比较。"],[289.8,295.99,173,"Here, for example, the words most similar to simple include straightforward and uncomplicated.","比如这里，与 simple 最相似的词包括 straightforward 和 uncomplicated。"],[296.34,300.0,173,"Try a familiar word first, then a rare name or a typo.","先试一个熟悉的词，再试一个罕见的名字或者一个拼写错误。"],[300.35,302.4,173,"Coverage can be the limiting factor.","覆盖率可能会成为限制因素。"],[302.75,306.21,173,"FastText can build representations from character pieces.","FastText 可以从字符片段构建表示。"],[306.56,315.83,173,"Static embeddings also give bank one vector across different senses, which helps explain the motivation for contextual representations later.","静态词向量还会让 bank 的不同义项共用一个向量，这也有助于解释后面引入上下文相关表示的动机。"],[317.08,321.4,174,"We have several methods now. How do we decide which vectors are better?","现在我们有好几种方法了。该如何判断哪组向量更好？"],[321.75,328.87,174,"We can check a specific word relationship, or we can put the vectors into a real system and measure the result.","我们可以检查某种特定的词间关系，也可以把向量放进一个真实系统里衡量效果。"],[329.22,331.44,174,"These answer different questions.","这两种方法回答的是不同的问题。"],[331.79,335.18,174,"Let's separate them before looking at the evaluation examples.","在看评估的例子之前，我们先把它们区分开。"],[336.43,340.76,175,"An intrinsic test might ask whether sofa is close to couch.","内在评估可能会问：sofa 是否和 couch 接近？"],[341.11,346.07,175,"An extrinsic test might ask whether the vectors improve review classification.","外在评估则可能问：这些向量能否提升评论分类的效果？"],[346.42,348.84,175,"The first is quick and informative.","前者快速且能提供信息。"],[349.19,352.09,175,"The second is closer to what the application needs.","后者更贴近应用的实际需求。"],[352.44,356.46,175,"Better similarity scores don't guarantee better task performance.","更好的相似度分数并不能保证更好的任务表现。"],[356.81,361.92,175,"For a fair comparison, keep the rest of the setup as consistent as possible.","为了公平比较，其余的设置应尽量保持一致。"],[363.17,368.85,176,"A simple baseline adds up the word vectors in a sentence, then trains a classifier on the sum.","一个简单的基线方法是把句子中的词向量加起来，再用这个和训练分类器。"],[369.2,374.77,176,"But think about, the dog chased the cat, and, the cat chased the dog.","但想一想 the dog chased the cat 和 the cat chased the dog 这两句话。"],[375.12,378.44,176,"Their sums are identical because the words are the same.","它们的向量和完全相同，因为用到的词一样。"],[378.79,380.29,176,"We lose word order.","我们丢失了词序。"],[380.64,382.54,176,"Negation can be difficult too.","否定也可能很难处理。"],[382.89,388.21,176,"Averaging instead of summing reduces the effect of sentence length, but it still loses order.","用平均代替求和可以减小句子长度的影响，但仍然会丢失词序。"],[388.56,393.83,176,"This baseline is useful, but it shows why richer ways of combining words matter.","这个基线很有用，但它也说明了为什么更丰富的词语组合方式很重要。"],[395.08,399.39,177,"Here we compare model scores with human ratings for word pairs.","这里我们把模型给词对打的分数和人类的评分进行比较。"],[399.74,403.2,177,"Should tiger and cat rank above stock and phone?","tiger 和 cat 是否应该排在 stock 和 phone 前面？"],[403.55,406.84,177,"The model scores each pair with cosine similarity.","模型用余弦相似度给每个词对打分。"],[407.19,410.73,177,"Spearman correlation checks whether the rankings agree.","Spearman 相关系数检查两者的排序是否一致。"],[411.08,415.73,177,"The model doesn't have to reproduce the exact human score scale.","模型不必复现人类评分的具体数值范围。"],[416.08,426.1,177,"Also, WordSim-353 includes association as well as similarity, so a high score doesn't mean the model has learned only synonymy.","另外，WordSim-353 既包含相似性也包含关联性，所以高分并不意味着模型只学到了同义关系。"],[427.35,433.02,178,"Read one column at a time, since each dataset tests a particular collection of pairs.","一次只看一列，因为每个数据集测试的是一组特定的词对。"],[433.37,437.4,178,"Then check the training data size before comparing methods.","然后在比较方法之前，先看看训练数据的规模。"],[437.75,443.11,178,"The bold results belong to the setup shown here, GloVe trained on forty-two billion tokens.","加粗的结果属于这里展示的设置，也就是在四百二十亿词元上训练的 GloVe。"],[443.46,446.09,178,"It doesn't establish a universal winner.","这并不能证明它在所有情况下都是赢家。"],[446.44,452.64,178,"Next we'll test a different property: whether the vectors capture relationships that support analogies.","接下来我们测试另一种性质：这些向量是否捕捉到了能支持类比推理的关系。"],[453.89,458.13,179,"Chicago is in Illinois. Philadelphia is in Pennsylvania.","芝加哥在伊利诺伊州，费城在宾夕法尼亚州。"],[458.48,463.22,179,"That's the semantic relation we're trying to transfer in the first example.","这就是第一个例子中我们要迁移的语义关系。"],[463.57,469.31,179,"For bad to worst, the relation is a superlative, so cool should lead us toward coolest.","对于 bad 到 worst，关系是最高级，所以 cool 应该把我们引向 coolest。"],[469.66,476.47,179,"We test this with vector arithmetic and a nearest-neighbor search, usually excluding the query words.","我们用向量运算加最近邻搜索来测试，通常会排除查询中的词。"],[476.82,478.45,179,"The metric is accuracy.","评价指标是准确率。"],[478.8,484.43,179,"It's intuitive, but it covers only part of what word meaning involves.","这很直观，但它只涵盖了词义的一部分。"],[485.68,492.73,180,"Here are analogy results for many models, split into semantic and syntactic subsets.","这里是许多模型的类比测试结果，分为语义和句法两个子集。"],[493.08,497.64,180,"The semantic and syntactic subsets can give different results.","语义子集和句法子集可能给出不同的结果。"],[497.99,501.55,180,"A model may do well on one and less well on the other.","一个模型可能在其中一个上表现很好，在另一个上则差一些。"],[501.9,508.93,180,"Vocabulary coverage matters too: the model can't retrieve an answer that isn't in its vocabulary.","词表覆盖率也很重要：模型无法检索出不在其词表中的答案。"],[509.28,514.52,180,"So use several checks, including the task you actually care about.","所以要使用多种检验，包括你真正关心的那个任务。"],[514.87,518.22,180,"That brings us to the end of the main material.","到这里，主要内容就讲完了。"]]},{"n":10,"title":"Wrap-Up and Appendix: Deriving Word2vec Gradients","blurb":"A recap of the lecture's thread from structure to representation, then a step-by-step derivation of the skip-gram gradients and the full-softmax training algorithm.","file":"lecture02_part10.mp4","poster":"lecture02_part10.jpg","duration":355.7,"first":181,"last":194,"slides":[{"n":181,"title":"Acknowledgements","t":0.8},{"n":182,"title":"Thank You","t":19.9},{"n":183,"title":"Appendix: Deriving the Gradients","t":48.89},{"n":184,"title":"Training Word2vec","t":67.55},{"n":185,"title":"Vectorized Gradients","t":90.1},{"n":186,"title":"Vectorized Gradient Exercise","t":112.54},{"n":187,"title":"Vectorized Gradient Exercise","t":139.31},{"n":188,"title":"Computing Word2vec Gradients","t":160.81},{"n":189,"title":"Computing Word2vec Gradients","t":186.57},{"n":190,"title":"Computing Word2vec Gradients","t":208.11},{"n":191,"title":"Computing Word2vec Gradients","t":233.25},{"n":192,"title":"Computing Word2vec Gradients","t":254.87},{"n":193,"title":"The Full-Softmax Training Algorithm","t":284.38},{"n":194,"title":"The Full-Softmax Training Algorithm","t":321.64}],"sents":[[0.8,7.01,181,"These courses and resources contributed many of the examples and explanations in this lecture.","这些课程和资料为本讲提供了许多例子和讲解。"],[7.36,11.81,181,"They offer useful follow-up reading if you'd like another treatment of the same ideas.","如果你想从另一个角度理解同样的内容，它们是很好的延伸阅读。"],[12.16,18.5,181,"We'll finish by connecting the main points, then leave the detailed gradient derivation in the appendix.","最后我们把要点串联起来，详细的梯度推导则留在附录中。"],[19.75,24.27,182,"We started with language structure and ended with learned representations.","我们从语言结构出发，最后来到了学习得到的表示。"],[24.62,30.01,182,"The connection was context: patterns of use give a model useful clues about words.","两者之间的纽带是上下文：用法的模式为模型提供了关于词的有用线索。"],[30.36,35.78,182,"Before we move on, can you explain why similar contexts can lead to similar vectors?","在继续之前，你能解释为什么相似的上下文会得到相似的向量吗？"],[36.13,39.41,182,"And why negative sampling reduces training cost?","以及为什么负采样能降低训练代价？"],[39.76,41.67,182,"Take a moment to answer these for yourself.","花点时间自己回答一下这两个问题。"],[42.02,47.49,182,"If you'd like to follow the calculus, the appendix works through the full-softmax gradients.","如果你想跟着推导微积分，附录里会完整推导完整 softmax 的梯度。"],[48.74,52.67,183,"This appendix picks up the Skip-gram objective from the main lecture.","这个附录接着正文中的 Skip-gram 目标函数继续讲。"],[53.02,57.46,183,"We'll work with one center-context pair first, then build the full update.","我们先处理一个中心词-上下文词对，再构建完整的更新。"],[57.81,60.47,183,"That keeps the algebra small enough to follow.","这样代数推导就足够简单，便于跟上。"],[60.82,66.15,183,"It also lets us see exactly why the full-softmax method touches so many parameters.","它也让我们能清楚地看到，为什么完整 softmax 方法会涉及如此多的参数。"],[67.4,73.64,184,"Think of theta as all the numbers in both embedding tables, collected into one long vector.","把 theta 看作两张词向量表中的所有数字，拼成的一个长向量。"],[73.99,77.82,184,"We want the gradient that tells us how to reduce the loss.","我们想要的是能告诉我们如何降低损失的梯度。"],[78.17,85.25,184,"Because the full loss is a sum, we can derive the contribution from one training pair and then accumulate contributions.","因为总损失是一个求和，我们可以先推导一个训练样本对的贡献，再把各个贡献累加起来。"],[85.6,88.7,184,"Let's review two simple derivatives before tackling that pair.","在处理这个词对之前，我们先复习两个简单的导数。"],[89.95,95.27,185,"For f of x equals x dot a, the gradient with respect to x is a.","对于 f 等于 x 点乘 a，它关于 x 的梯度就是 a。"],[95.62,98.18,185,"To see why, expand the dot product.","要理解原因，把点积展开。"],[98.53,105.13,185,"Each term is x sub i times a sub i, so its derivative with respect to x sub i is a sub i.","每一项是 x i 乘以 a i，所以它关于 x i 的导数就是 a i。"],[105.48,108.55,185,"Stack those derivatives and you get the vector a.","把这些导数堆叠起来，就得到了向量 a。"],[108.9,111.14,185,"Next we'll add an exponential.","接下来我们加入一个指数函数。"],[112.39,117.31,186,"Try differentiating exp of w dot x with respect to x.","试着对 exp 的 w 点乘 x 关于 x 求导。"],[117.66,119.56,186,"What shape should the answer have?","答案应该是什么形状？"],[119.91,121.78,186,"Take a moment to work it out.","花点时间算一算。"],[122.13,127.89,186,"The output is a scalar and x is a vector, so the gradient must be a vector.","输出是标量，而 x 是向量，所以梯度必须是一个向量。"],[128.24,131.6,186,"That already rules out a scalar-only answer.","这就已经排除了只是一个标量的答案。"],[131.95,135.4,186,"Now apply the chain rule to the exponential and the inner product.","现在对指数函数和内积应用链式法则。"],[135.75,137.91,186,"The next slide checks the result.","下一页来核对结果。"],[139.16,143.51,187,"The answer is c: exp of w dot x, multiplied by w.","答案是 c：exp 的 w 点乘 x，再乘以 w。"],[143.86,149.61,187,"For each coordinate, the inner derivative contributes the corresponding entry of w.","对每个坐标而言，内层导数贡献的是 w 中对应的那个元素。"],[149.96,152.52,187,"This is the pattern we'll need inside softmax.","这正是我们在 softmax 里需要用到的模式。"],[152.87,156.71,187,"Once you've recognized it, the longer expression becomes easier to handle.","一旦认出了这个模式，更长的表达式就更容易处理了。"],[157.06,159.41,187,"Let's return to one observed word pair.","让我们回到一个观察到的词对上。"],[160.66,164.28,188,"Fix a center t and an observed context c.","固定一个中心词 t 和一个观察到的上下文词 c。"],[164.63,169.79,188,"The loss is the negative log probability of that context.","损失就是这个上下文词的负对数概率。"],[170.14,174.56,188,"We need the gradient for the center vector and for the context vectors.","我们需要求关于中心向量和上下文向量的梯度。"],[174.91,181.15,188,"Even unobserved context words appear in the denominator, so they can receive gradients too.","即使是没有观察到的上下文词也出现在分母中，所以它们同样会得到梯度。"],[181.5,185.17,188,"First, let's rewrite the expression in a more convenient form.","首先，我们把这个表达式改写成更方便的形式。"],[186.42,188.6,189,"Use the log rule for a quotient.","使用商的对数法则。"],[188.95,193.39,189,"The log of the exponential in the numerator becomes the dot product itself.","分子中指数的对数就变成了点积本身。"],[193.74,202.47,189,"That leaves two terms: a negative dot product for the observed pair, plus log of the sum of exponentiated scores.","这样就剩下两项：观察到的词对的负点积，加上所有指数化分数之和的对数。"],[202.82,206.71,189,"We can now differentiate those two pieces separately.","现在我们可以分别对这两部分求导。"],[207.96,211.32,190,"The first term gives us negative v sub c.","第一项给出负的 v c。"],[211.67,213.65,190,"For the second, call the sum Z.","对于第二项，把那个求和记作 Z。"],[214.0,219.68,190,"The derivative of log Z is one over Z, multiplied by the derivative of Z.","log Z 的导数是 Z 分之一，再乘以 Z 的导数。"],[220.03,224.58,190,"Now differentiate each exponential using the rule we just practiced.","现在用我们刚练习过的法则，对每个指数项求导。"],[224.93,229.72,190,"The result looks long, but there's a familiar probability hiding in it.","结果看起来很长，但里面藏着一个熟悉的概率。"],[230.07,231.85,190,"We'll identify it next.","我们接下来就把它找出来。"],[233.1,240.83,191,"Look at this fraction: an exponentiated score divided by the sum of all exponentiated scores.","看这个分式：一个指数化的分数，除以所有指数化分数之和。"],[241.18,243.2,191,"That's exactly P of k given t.","这恰好就是给定 t 时 k 的概率。"],[243.55,248.35,191,"Replacing the fraction with its probability name makes the derivative easier to read.","把这个分式换成它的概率记号，导数就更容易读懂了。"],[248.7,253.47,191,"It also helps us interpret the result, which the next slide highlights.","这也有助于我们解读结果，下一页会重点展示。"],[254.72,258.15,192,"The boxed fraction is exactly P of k given t.","框出来的这个分式恰好就是给定 t 时 k 的概率。"],[258.5,264.27,192,"So the center gradient is the model's average context vector minus the observed context vector.","所以中心向量的梯度，就是模型的平均上下文向量减去观察到的上下文向量。"],[264.62,267.87,192,"The average uses the model's predicted probabilities as weights.","这个平均以模型预测的概率作为权重。"],[268.22,274.79,192,"Subtracting this gradient moves the center toward the observed context relative to that average.","减去这个梯度，会让中心向量相对于这个平均值，朝观察到的上下文移动。"],[275.14,280.75,192,"If the observed context already gets nearly all the probability, the terms nearly cancel.","如果观察到的上下文已经占据了几乎全部概率，这两项就几乎相互抵消。"],[281.1,282.98,192,"Now let's put the updates together.","现在我们把这些更新组合起来。"],[284.23,292.68,193,"The algorithm takes a text corpus, an embedding size d, a vocabulary, and a context size m, and initializes all vectors randomly.","这个算法的输入是文本语料、嵌入维度 d、词表和上下文大小 m，并随机初始化所有向量。"],[293.03,300.89,193,"For a context vector, the gradient has the familiar form, prediction minus target, multiplied by the center vector.","对于上下文向量，梯度具有熟悉的形式：预测值减去目标值，再乘以中心向量。"],[301.24,305.46,193,"The target is one for the observed context and zero for the others.","对观察到的上下文，目标值是一；对其他词，目标值是零。"],[305.81,309.02,193,"Each update subtracts the learning rate times the gradient.","每次更新都减去学习率乘以梯度。"],[309.37,314.62,193,"Compute the pair's gradients consistently before changing the parameters.","在修改参数之前，要先一致地算出这个词对的所有梯度。"],[314.97,317.92,193,"Notice how every context row is involved.","注意，每一个上下文向量行都参与了进来。"],[318.27,320.24,193,"That leads to our final question.","这就引出了我们最后一个问题。"],[321.49,324.23,194,"Which part makes this algorithm expensive?","这个算法中，是哪一部分让它代价高昂？"],[324.58,326.22,194,"Take a moment to think about it.","花点时间想一想。"],[326.57,331.8,194,"The denominator and the context updates both scale with the vocabulary size.","分母和上下文向量的更新，都随词表大小而增长。"],[332.15,336.67,194,"The cost is roughly vocabulary size times d for each pair.","每个词对的代价大约是词表大小乘以 d。"],[337.02,342.41,194,"With negative sampling, it is roughly K times d instead, and K is small.","使用负采样后，代价大约变成 K 乘以 d，而 K 很小。"],[342.76,349.33,194,"So the calculus brings us back to the practical motivation: we want a useful learning signal at a much lower cost.","所以，这些微积分推导又把我们带回了实际的动机：我们希望以低得多的代价获得有用的学习信号。"],[349.68,353.89,194,"That's all for today's lecture. Thank you, and see you next time!","今天的课就到这里。谢谢大家，我们下次见！"]]}]};
